</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#005B96;" stroke="#005B96" stroke-width="0.8pt"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 2.98 -2.98)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M -90.64 -152.96 h 37.09 v 37.09 h -37.09 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#005B96;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#005B96" stroke-width="0.8pt"><path d="M -58.31 -134.41 C -58.31 -126.8 -64.48 -120.63 -72.09 -120.63 C -79.7 -120.63 -85.87 -126.8 -85.87 -134.41 C -85.87 -142.02 -79.7 -148.19 -72.09 -148.19 C -64.48 -148.19 -58.31 -142.02 -58.31 -134.41 Z M -72.09 -134.41"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#005B96;--ltx-fill-color:#005B96;" fill="#005B96" stroke="#005B96" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -75.78 -138.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.54em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10.65pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="7.37"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.F1.pic1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#005B96;">6</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#005B96;--ltx-fill-color:#005B96;" fill="#005B96" stroke="#005B96" stroke-width="2.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 72.09 0 L 99.06 0"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#005B96;" color="#005B96" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-width="1.99165pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 99.06 0)"><path d="M 7.58 0 C 6.65 0.26 2.56 1.73 0 3.33 L 0 -3.33 C 2.56 -1.73 6.65 -0.26 7.58 0 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#005B96;--ltx-fill-color:#005B96;" fill="#005B96" stroke="#005B96" stroke-width="2.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 255.65 0 L 282.62 0"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#005B96;" color="#005B96" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-width="1.99165pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 282.62 0)"><path d="M 7.58 0 C 6.65 0.26 2.56 1.73 0 3.33 L 0 -3.33 C 2.56 -1.73 6.65 -0.26 7.58 0 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#005B96;--ltx-fill-color:#005B96;" fill="#005B96" stroke="#005B96" stroke-width="2.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 367.12 -63.55 L 367.12 -122.01"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#005B96;" color="#005B96" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-width="1.99165pt" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 367.12 -122.01)"><path d="M 7.58 0 C 6.65 0.26 2.56 1.73 0 3.33 L 0 -3.33 C 2.56 -1.73 6.65 -0.26 7.58 0 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#005B96;--ltx-fill-color:#005B96;" fill="#005B96" stroke="#005B96" stroke-width="2.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 295.02 -197.96 L 268.06 -197.96"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#005B96;" color="#005B96" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-width="1.99165pt" transform="matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 -1.0 268.06 -197.96)"><path d="M 7.58 0 C 6.65 0.26 2.56 1.73 0 3.33 L 0 -3.33 C 2.56 -1.73 6.65 -0.26 7.58 0 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#005B96;--ltx-fill-color:#005B96;" fill="#005B96" stroke="#005B96" stroke-width="2.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 111.46 -197.96 L 84.5 -197.96"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#005B96;" color="#005B96" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-width="1.99165pt" transform="matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 -1.0 84.5 -197.96)"><path d="M 7.58 0 C 6.65 0.26 2.56 1.73 0 3.33 L 0 -3.33 C 2.56 -1.73 6.65 -0.26 7.58 0 Z"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Schematic overview of our empirical study workflow.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Repository Selection</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To curate a representative, high-quality dataset of agentic AI repositories, we followed the repository selection methodology from prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rahardja et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. We queried GitHub for repositories associated with the term “AI agents” that were active as of June 2025. To focus on robust, widely used systems, we retained repositories with more than 1,000 stars and at least 30 issues. These thresholds target projects with sustained maintenance and community engagement, filtering out transient or inactive repositories. This screening yielded 82 candidates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We then applied a language constraint to align with the dominant agent-development ecosystem: because Python is the predominant language for agentic workflows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hasan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we excluded repositories implemented in other languages. This reduced the candidate set from 82 to 47.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">After automated filtering, we conducted manual annotation to ensure that the dataset comprised only agentic AI frameworks, libraries, or tools. To assess inter-rater agreement, we used Cohen’s Kappa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Díaz et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, a standard measure in similar studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morovati et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. In the first round, two annotators independently labelled all repositories, yielding a Cohen’s Kappa of 0.32 (fair agreement) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">McHugh, 2012</a>)</cite>. The annotators then reconciled disagreements and refined inclusion criteria. A second round produced a Cohen’s Kappa of 0.83 (substantial agreement). This process resulted in a final dataset of 40 Agentic AI repositories.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Filtering the Issues</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After selecting repositories, we curated a clean set of closed issues and their corresponding merged pull requests. Closed issues provide evidence of observed failure symptoms and execution contexts, while merged pull requests supply validated fault-localisation and repair information. This pairing enables analysis of both the manifestation of failure and its underlying root causes. We first applied keyword-based filtering to all closed issues and merged PRs, following established approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abidi et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morovati et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, yielding 24,193 candidate items. Following repository mining practices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kalliamvakou et al., 2014</a>)</cite>, we excluded items written in languages other than English (a small fraction was in Chinese <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Munaiah et al., 2017</a>)</cite>). We also removed items associated with example or test sections of repositories and entries that did not involve Python files or documentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kalliamvakou et al., 2014</a>)</cite>. Applying these criteria reduced the dataset to 19,947 candidate closed issues and merged PRs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the reliability of the automated filtering, two of the authors manually annotated a representative sample of items (i.e., 68 issues/PRs) from the dataset above. This assessment revealed that 68.6% of the sampled entries were truly related to a fault, while the remaining 32.4% constituted noise or non-bug-related items. They included user mistakes (e.g., incorrect environment setup) (e.g., <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crewAIInc/crewAI#1867</span>), non-reproducible issues (e.g., <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen#4063</span>), and feature requests (e.g., <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb/mindsdb#4721</span>). Although the initial filtering improved dataset quality, the remaining noise indicated the need for an additional refinement step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To reduce noise in our dataset, we used GPT-4.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite> as a final filtering step, consistent with prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. First, we validated GPT-4.1 against a ground-truth set of 68 manually labelled items; it achieved 83% accuracy and 97% recall in detecting bug-fix issues and PRs. We then manually reviewed a separate sample of 68 items and found GPT-4.1’s judgments matching human interpretation in 85.29% of cases. We noted that non-bug items included enhancements (e.g., <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack#1453</span>), stale reports (e.g., <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">eosphoros-ai/DB-GPT#1274</span>), and irreproducible issues (e.g., <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Arize-ai/phoenix#7590</span>). Using GPT-4.1, we thus removed all items classified as non-bug-related, yielding 13,602 closed issues and merged PRs for our analysis. This multi-stage process combined human annotation with AI-assisted validation to produce a high-quality dataset of bug-related activities in agentic AI repositories.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Dataset Construction</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To reflect the diversity of the agentic AI ecosystem and support generalisability, we constructed a corpus of 40 repositories through a category-agnostic search process, screening each candidate for relevance to agentic systems without imposing architectural constraints during discovery. Once the final set of repositories was established, we conducted open coding to classify each repository according to its primary architectural role. This classification was guided by the IEEE Standard Glossary of Software Engineering Terminology and established repository taxonomies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. This process yielded four categories—<span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Frameworks</span>, <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Applications</span>, <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Libraries</span>, and <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tools</span>—which were used solely to characterise ecosystem coverage rather than to guide repository selection or issue retrieval. The Cohen’s kappa for the process was 0.86, which indicates near-perfect agreement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">McHugh, 2012</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Because our filtered corpus comprises 13,602 items, conducting a full manual analysis was infeasible. We therefore employed stratified random sampling to construct a statistically representative subset. The target sample size was set to 385, which yields a 95% confidence level with a 5% margin of error, a commonly accepted threshold in empirical software engineering studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To preserve the underlying distribution, we applied stratified sampling across the four categories so that each category’s share in the sample matched its share in the population. The final dataset contains 385 faults (issues + PRs): <span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">234</span> from Frameworks, <span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">75</span> from Libraries, <span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">39</span> from Tools, and <span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">37</span> from Applications.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4. </span>Grounded Theory and Taxonomy Development</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To develop a comprehensive taxonomy of faults in agentic AI systems, we employed a grounded theory approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Glaser and Strauss, 2017</a>)</cite> for our analysis. This inductive approach derives categories directly from empirical data rather than relying on an existing taxonomy. We manually examined all 385 sampled items in detail, focussing on reported fault types, observable symptoms, and documented root causes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To reduce subjectivity and maintain consistency, the analysis was conducted iteratively by the first and second authors. We reviewed the items in batches of 25 and met after each batch to clarify ambiguous cases, resolve disagreements, and refine shared coding guidelines. This process allowed the coding scheme to evolve as new agent-specific fault modes emerged, such as errors from autonomous reasoning loops (e.g., <span id="S2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen#4020</span>) or failures in LLM integration pathways (e.g., <span id="S2.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen#4770</span>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We developed the taxonomy through a three-stage coding process as follows:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Open Coding:</span> We assign descriptive labels to the raw artefacts, including issue descriptions, execution logs, stack traces, and corresponding fix commits. These labels captured fine-grained technical phenomena, such as <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">API parameter mismatches</span> (e.g., <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">letta-ai/letta#517</span>), <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">token-limit overflows</span> (e.g., <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen#4770</span>), and <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dependency version conflicts</span> (e.g., <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crewAIInc/crewAI#1877</span>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Axial Coding:</span> We then examined relationships among these labels to identify conceptual connections and shared underlying causes. Related open codes were grouped into broader sub-categories that reflected operation of agentic AI systems. For example, LLM configuration and API token-handling faults were consolidated under LLM integration problems, while data-type and encoding-related faults were grouped as interpretive errors.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Selective Coding:</span> Finally, we synthesised these sub-categories into higher-level dimensions aligned with major architectural components of agentic AI systems, such as the cognitive control loop, tool and environment interaction layer and the runtime environment.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Through this process, we distilled 385 individual faults into a coherent taxonomy. The resulting structure captures both low-level technical faults and broader architectural issues, reflecting distinctive failure modes ranging from breakdowns in internal reasoning to integration errors and vulnerabilities in the operating environment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5. </span>Apriori Analysis</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To identify systematic relationships among fault categories, observable symptoms, and root causes, we used the Apriori algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agrawal and Srikant, 1994</a>)</cite>. Apriori is a widely used data mining technique that finds frequent itemsets in transactional data and generates association rules describing how attributes co-occur. In a rule, the <span id="S2.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">antecedent</span> denotes the observed condition(s), and the <span id="S2.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">consequent</span> denotes the attribute(s) that tend to co-occur with those conditions within the same fault instances. In our context, the antecedent can be a fault category or a symptom, and the consequent can be a symptom or a root cause. For example, a rule of the form
<span id="S2.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Symptom = Redundant Tracking</span> <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="S2.SS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Root Cause = Wrong Default Component Configuration</span>
indicates that faults exhibiting redundant tracking frequently have this specific root cause in our dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We encoded each of the 385 analysed faults as a transaction with three categorical attributes: <span id="S2.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fault Category</span>, <span id="S2.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Symptom</span>, and <span id="S2.SS5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Root Cause</span>. For example, the following transaction indicates that an <span id="S2.SS5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM Configuration</span> fault can manifest as <span id="S2.SS5.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Redundant Token Tracking</span> and is caused by a <span id="S2.SS5.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Wrong Default Component Config</span> (e.g., <span id="S2.SS5.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack#4944</span>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textit{Fault Category}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Fault Category</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textit{Fault Category}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{LLM Configuration}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mtext>LLM Configuration</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{LLM Configuration},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex1Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textit{Symptom}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Symptom</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textit{Symptom}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{Redundant Tracking}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mtext>Redundant Tracking</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{Redundant Tracking},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex1Xb"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textit{Root Cause}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Root Cause</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textit{Root Cause}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{Wrong Default Component Configuration}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mtext>Wrong Default Component Configuration</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{Wrong Default Component Configuration}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify the strength and reliability of each rule, we computed standard association metrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dasseni et al., 2001</a>)</cite>:</p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Support</span> measures how frequently items in the antecedent and the consequent co-occur in the dataset. For a rule <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>, support is defined as the proportion of transactions in which both <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> appear together.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Confidence</span> measures how likely the consequent is to occur when the antecedent is present. Formally, for a rule <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>, confidence is the conditional probability that a transaction containing <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> also contains <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By filtering for high-confidence rules, we extracted a concise set of associations that summarise relationships among faults, symptoms, and root causes. For instance, if we observe a high-confidence rule such as
<span id="S2.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Symptom = Execution Loop</span> <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="S2.SS5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Root Cause = Incorrect Termination Condition</span>.
This suggests that, in our dataset, faults that exhibit execution loops are frequently associated with termination misconfigurations. While these rules do not establish causality, they provide empirical evidence to guide root-cause investigation by highlighting statistically significant symptom–cause associations. These rules provide a systematic way to infer likely causes from observed symptoms, improving the interpretability and practical utility of the taxonomy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.6. </span>Developer Study</h3>

<div id="S2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the comprehensiveness of our proposed taxonomy, we conducted a qualitative survey with practitioners actively developing agentic AI systems. The survey evaluates whether the taxonomy captures the real-world faults that they routinely encounter.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS6.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.6.1. </span>Instrument Design</h4>

<div id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To gather structured feedback from the participants, we designed an online questionnaire targeting different aspects of our proposed taxonomy. The survey was divided into three sections. The first section collected <span id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">demographic information</span>, including participants’ current professional role, total years of experience in software engineering, and experience with agentic AI frameworks such as AutoGen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Microsoft, 2024</a>)</cite>, LangChain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">LangChain, 2024</a>)</cite>, and BabyAGI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">BabyAGI, 2024</a>)</cite>. This information allowed us to assess respondents’ expertise and ensure that feedback came from individuals with relevant experience.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The second section focused on <span id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">taxonomy evaluation</span>. Participants reviewed the full hierarchical taxonomy and rated their agreement on a five-point Likert scale at each level of granularity — i.e, for every top-level category, subcategory, and individual fault type. This provided fine-grained quantitative evidence of alignment between the taxonomy and practitioners’ experience of agentic faults across all levels, rather than only at an aggregate or structural level.
The final section collected <span id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">open-ended feedback</span> on completeness, inviting participants to describe missing faults, symptoms, or root causes and suggest additions or refinements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Before administering the main survey, we conducted a pilot study with two academic researchers and two industry practitioners. Feedback from the pilot led to refinements in category definitions to reduce ambiguity and improve interpretability. For example, we revised several category descriptions to include clearer boundary conditions and short illustrative examples, ensuring that similar but distinct concepts were more explicitly differentiated. We estimated the survey would take approximately 10–15 minutes. The full questionnaire is available in the replication package <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah, 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.6.2. </span>Participant Selection</h4>

<div id="S2.SS6.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure high-quality and relevant responses, we employed targeted recruitment. First, we identified active contributors to the selected repositories. Using public commit histories, we extracted the public email addresses of top contributors and sent personalised invitations. This ensured participants were familiar with practical challenges in agentic AI development.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Second, to broaden reach, we disseminated the survey through online communities frequented by AI developers, including specialised Discord servers and Reddit forums dedicated to LLMs and autonomous agents. This combination of direct recruitment and community outreach helped us capture feedback from the individuals with relevant experience.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.6.3. </span>Demographics</h4>

<div id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A total of 145 participants completed the survey, representing diverse professional backgrounds, experience levels, and framework expertise. By <span id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">affiliation</span>, 76.1% were from industry and 23.9% from academia. Among them, 29.0% reported more than 5 years of experience in software engineering, and 47.8% reported at least 1 year of experience in agentic AI systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Participants reported familiarity with a range of agentic AI frameworks. The most common were LangChain (69.6%) and AutoGen (34.8%), with additional experience across other frameworks. This distribution indicates broad cross-framework familiarity, providing a solid basis for evaluating our taxonomy’s completeness, accuracy, and usability.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.7. </span>Analysis of Developer Study Results</h3>

<div id="S2.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analysed the developer study responses using complementary quantitative and qualitative methods to evaluate the taxonomy systematically.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS7.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Quantitative analysis.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Participants rated the practical relevance of each taxonomy element on a five-point Likert scale (1 = not relevant, 5 = highly relevant) at all levels of the hierarchy, including dimensions, categories, subcategories, and individual fault types. For each element, we computed descriptive statistics (mean, median, and standard deviation) and agreement proportions (ratings <math id="S2.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>). We aggregated ratings at subcategory, category, and dimension levels to examine consistency across the hierarchical structure. To assess deviation from neutrality, we compared mean ratings against the midpoint of the scale (3.0) using one-sample tests on aggregated ratings. We also examined rating distributions across experience strata (e.g., years of agentic AI development) to assess stability across respondent groups. We evaluated internal consistency of ratings across taxonomy elements using Cronbach’s <math id="S2.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS7.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Qualitative analysis.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analysed the open-ended responses to identify limitations in taxonomy coverage and specification. Two authors independently reviewed all responses and applied inductive thematic coding to segments describing missing, unclear, or misclassified faults. They compared initial codes, resolved discrepancies through discussion, and iteratively refined a shared coding scheme. We then grouped the agreed codes into higher-level themes representing recurring coverage gaps, definition ambiguities, and structural placement issues within the taxonomy hierarchy. The resulting themes and representative examples are reported in RQ3. The authors resolved remaining disagreements through consensus.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Study Results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents the findings of our study by answering three research questions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S3.F2.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.F2.2.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.F2.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:3.3pt;height:0pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<span id="S3.F2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="S3.F2.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_ERROR">{forest}</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Taxonomy of Bugs in Agentic Systems. The numbers in parentheses indicate the frequency of faults.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>RQ1: What are the characteristics, observable symptoms, and root causes of bugs and failures in real-world agentic AI systems?</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis of 385 real-world faults demonstrates that failures in agentic AI systems differ fundamentally from those in traditional software systems. Their differences are driven by the architectural demands of autonomy: agents must (i) configure and integrate an LLM-based cognitive core, (ii) maintain long-lived state across iterative control loops, and (iii) invoke external tools and services in dynamic environments. To characterise such a fault landscape, we developed a taxonomy comprising 37 fault categories grouped into 13 major categories. We also organised them into five high-level dimensions that correspond to core agent capabilities required for autonomy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1. </span>Taxonomy of Fault Types</h4>

</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">I. Agent Cognition &amp; Orchestration (83 faults)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This dimension captures faults intrinsic to an agent’s cognitive architecture and control flows. Unlike conventional software failures, these failures often arise from the interaction between the model behaviour and the orchestration logic of an agentic system.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">1. LLM Integration Faults (45 faults)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This category represents issues or defects at the interface between an agent framework and an LLM provider, including misconfiguration, API incompatibilities, and token management issues.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I1" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I1.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM Misconfiguration:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises when the static configuration of an LLM provider or model is incorrect or inconsistent. It includes incorrect model identifiers, invalid parameter values (e.g., temperature or context length), incorrect file paths, or mismatched provider settings. Such misconfigurations prevent an agent from accessing or invoking the LLM. This fault can lead to failures such as runtime exceptions during prompt or template loading, invalid or malformed model responses, or unsuccessful LLM initialisation. For example, <span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">letta-ai/letta</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/letta-ai/letta/issues/180" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #180</a>) specified incorrect file paths in its LLM configuration, causing a <span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FileNotFoundError</span> during persona template retrieval. Similarly, <span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FoundationAgents/MetaGPT</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT/issues/1336" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1336</a>) produced invalid JSON responses when its RAG module interacted incorrectly with the Ollama model provider.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I1.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM Usage and API Incompatibility:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault occurs when an agent’s LLM invocation logic is incompatible with the provider’s API schema, protocol, or behavioural contract. It can be triggered by outdated client assumptions about request/response formats, streaming behaviour, tool-calling interfaces, or provider capabilities. Such incompatibilities arise from API evolution, schema drift, or inconsistent abstraction layers between the agent framework and LLM provider. This fault can lead to failures such as rejected API calls, incorrect tool execution, malformed responses, or broken agent workflows. For instance, <span id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/pull/2718" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #2718</a>) experienced failing assistant tests after an OpenAI API change that broke backward compatibility. Similarly, in <span id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">agno-agi/agno</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/agno-agi/agno/issues/2336" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2336</a>), streaming incompatibilities with provider APIs triggered redundant tool execution.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I1.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Token Handling &amp; Tracking Error:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises from a poor management or accounting of tokens within the agent framework. It includes inaccurate token counting, the use of different tokenizers by the agent framework and the LLM provider, or improper tracking of context windows across multiple turns. Because token limits govern prompt construction and conversation state, such defects compromise an agent’s reasoning stability and resource control. This fault can lead to various errors (e.g., context overflow, truncated prompts, inaccurate token usage metrics, or inconsistent multi-turn behaviour). For example, <span id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/issues/2702" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2702</a>) suffered from an improper token tracking problem for <span id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gpt-4o</span> due to mismatches between its observability-level accounting and provider tokenisation. Similarly, <span id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Arize-ai/phoenix</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/pull/5976" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #5976</a>) miscounted tokens because it used an inappropriate token counter type.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">2. Agent Lifecycle &amp; State (38 faults)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise from defects in an agent’s execution, internal state, and termination conditions. Unlike conventional programs with bounded control flow, agents operate autonomously, making reliable execution and state coherence essential to their reliability.</p>
<dl id="S3.I2" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I2.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agent Execution Failure:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault occurs when an agent’s core execution loop or orchestration logic is incorrectly implemented or configured. It includes defects in scheduling, task delegation, run configuration, or control sequencing that prevent intended actions from being executed in the correct order or at the correct frequency. Such defects can disrupt the agent’s operational flow. This fault can lead to failures such as agents not executing assigned tasks, duplicated executions, or incorrect action ordering. For example, <span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crewAIInc/crewAI</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI/issues/1501" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1501</a>) prevented coworker agents from executing due to defects in task delegation logic. In <span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">langflow-ai/langflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow/pull/2594" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #2594</a>), an incorrect run configuration in the execution pipeline caused the build cycle to run multiple times, leading to redundant builds, increased latency, and duplicate tool calls.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I2.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agent State Inconsistency:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault emerges when the internal state that defines an agent’s identity, context, or interaction history becomes corrupted or incorrectly updated. It includes defects in maintaining consistency between requests and their corresponding responses, between stored conversation state and the actual interaction history, between role assignments and active participants, and across parallel interaction contexts. Because agents rely on persistent state across turns, such inconsistencies compromise behavioural continuity. This fault can lead to failures such as mismatched responses, loss of conversational context, or inconsistent behaviour across turns. For example, <span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FoundationAgents/MetaGPT</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT/pull/1381" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #1381</a>) produced incoherent responses after losing the mapping between queries and outputs. Similarly, <span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/pull/5352" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #5352</a>) exhibited inconsistent assistant behaviour due to improper state management across multiple turns.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I2.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I2.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agent Termination Failure:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I2.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault occurs when the agent’s termination logic is defective or insufficient. It includes missing or incorrect stop criteria, improperly handled termination signals, or hard-coded termination logic that does not generalise across scenarios. Such defects prevent controlled shutdown of long-running agents. This fault can lead to failures such as infinite execution loops, uncontrolled resource consumption, or premature task termination. For instance, <span id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/issues/4020" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #4020</a>) lacked a proper termination mechanism in <span id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AssistantAgent</span>, leading to an ad hoc termination logic with limited generalisability.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">II. Tooling, Integration &amp; Actuation (66 faults)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This dimension captures faults in the enactment of plans by agents through tools, APIs, and external systems. Because agents translate probabilistic decisions into deterministic operations, defects at these system boundaries frequently disrupt execution and integration with external services.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">1. Tool Execution &amp; API Usage (17 faults)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise from an incorrect construction or invocation of external tools, preventing agents from interacting correctly with external interfaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I3" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I3.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">API Misuse:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises when the agent violates the semantic or behavioural contract of an API or tool interface. It includes calling unsupported API endpoints, supplying invalid parameters, or invoking operations beyond allowed ones. This fault can lead to failures such as rejected requests, tool execution errors, or incorrect external actions. For example, <span id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/pull/4703" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #4703</a>) violated query-builder constraints of <span id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Weaviate BM25</span> by passing unsupported arguments, which resulted in a program crash.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I3.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">API Parameter Mismatch:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises when supplied API parameters are incorrectly structured, typed, ordered, or encoded. It includes mismatches between expected and provided parameter schemas. This fault can lead to failures such as tool-calling errors, malformed requests, or unsuccessful API operations. For instance, <span id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SWE-agent/SWE-agent</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/SWE-agent/SWE-agent/issues/1159" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1159</a>) exhibited tool-calling failures due to incorrectly constructed tool-call arguments (e.g., null values where structured parameters were expected).</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I3.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I3.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">API Misconfiguration:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I3.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises when the static configuration used to invoke an API or tool is incorrect or inconsistent. It includes wrong base URLs, incorrect API versions, misconfigured headers, invalid timeouts, or incorrect tool endpoint configuration. They lead to failures, even if the agent constructs the request correctly. The failures include persistent request rejection, connection errors, or tool calls that consistently target the wrong service or API. Such misconfiguration is distinct from parameter mismatch because it reflects an incorrect tool setup rather than an incorrect tool call.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">2. External Connectivity &amp; Access (19 faults)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise from incorrect configurations or inappropriate handling of network connections and credentials required to access external systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I4" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I4.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I4.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Connection Setup Failure:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I4.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault occurs when incorrect connection parameters are used. It includes invalid endpoints, incorrect paths, or misconfigured secure bundles that prevent the establishment of external network connections. This fault can lead to various failures such as unreachable services and aborted operations. For example, <span id="S3.I4.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb/mindsdb</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/mindsdb/mindsdb/pull/7410" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #7410</a>) experienced broken Cassandra connections because the <span id="S3.I4.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">secure_connect_bundle</span> setting was given as an incorrect file path in the Cassandra connection configuration, which prevented the driver from establishing the connection.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I4.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I4.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Authentication or Authorisation Failure:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I4.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault occurs when access to a protected resource fails due to incorrect client credentials, misconfigured server-side authentication, or misconfigured permission checks. It includes missing or invalid credentials supplied by the client, as well as incorrect permission or access-control settings enforced by the service provider. This fault can lead to denied access, blocked operations, or application errors when attempting restricted actions. For example, the <span id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Arize-ai/phoenix</span> playground (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/pull/5003" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #5003</a>) crashed due to missing credential handling in its request flow. Similarly, <span id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">browser-use/browser-use</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/browser-use/browser-use/issues/2571" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2571</a>) reported permission-denied errors when attempting to access hardware peripherals without the required permissions.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">3. Resource Manipulation (16 faults)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise from incorrect acquisition, coordination, or configuration of persistent or shared resources used during tool-mediated execution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I5" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I5.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I5.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Resource Handling Error:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I5.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault occurs when shared resources such as threads, files, or locks are incorrectly managed or synchronised. It includes improper locking, release ordering defects, or unsafe concurrent access. This fault can lead to failures such as race conditions, execution errors, or corrupted shared state. For example, <span id="S3.I5.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">qodo-ai/pr-agent</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/qodo-ai/pr-agent/issues/1324" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1324</a>) experienced CI/CD failures due to inappropriate synchronisation during thread management.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I5.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I5.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Database Misconfiguration:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I5.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises when database connection or configuration settings are incorrect or inconsistent with the expected usage. It includes invalid connection parameters, storage back-end mismatches, or improper database options. This fault can lead to failures such as failed queries, unavailable storage, or aborted tool operations. For example, <span id="S3.I5.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">agno-agi/agno</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/agno-agi/agno/issues/1189" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1189</a>) failed during hybrid search with LanceDB due to improper database configuration.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">4. System Coordination (6 faults)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise from incorrect coordination of concurrent or distributed execution within the agent system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I6" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I6.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I6.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Synchronisation Error:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I6.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Synchronisation errors arise when parallel or asynchronous operations across agent components are incorrectly ordered or coordinated. Although asynchronous execution is nondeterministic in timing, correct systems enforce deterministic alignment of observable effects (e.g., request–response pairs or stream sequences). These faults occur when such coordination is missing or incorrect, leading to race conditions, misordered streamed outputs, or inconsistent handling of parallel agent steps, and resulting in misaligned outputs or interleaved execution. For example, <span id="S3.I6.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FoundationAgents/MetaGPT</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT/pull/1381" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #1381</a>) produced misaligned streamed outputs due to incorrect coordination of parallel response streams.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">5. System Observability (8 faults)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise from defects in the system’s intended observability instrumentation that reduce its ability to record or expose execution signals required for monitoring and diagnosis. They concern cases where logging, tracing, or metric emission is expected or designed but is missing, incorrect, or inconsistent, rather than intentionally disabled by configuration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I7" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I7.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I7.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Logging or Telemetry Defect:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I7.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I7.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises when intended logging, tracing, or metric instrumentation is missing, incorrect, or incomplete. It includes absent or malformed logs, incorrect metric emission, or inconsistent observability hooks where observability behaviour is expected. This fault can lead to undiagnosable errors, misleading monitoring outputs, or loss of execution visibility. For instance, <span id="S3.I7.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">langflow-ai/langflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow/pull/8457" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #8457</a>) required fixes to ensure correct metric logging for its Astra DB integration.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">III. Perception, Context &amp; Memory (72 faults)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This dimension comprises faults in how agents ingest information, preserve historical context, and interpret heterogeneous inputs. Agentic pipelines frequently combine unstructured LLM outputs with typed program logic, making defects at representation and interpretation boundaries a major source of behavioural breakdown.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">1. Context &amp; State Persistence (12 faults)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This category concerns defects in long-term storage and retrieval of interaction context across turns and multi-step tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx3.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I8" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I8.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I8.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory Persistence Failure:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I8.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault occurs when mechanisms that store or update conversational or task context are incorrectly implemented. It includes incorrect timestamp updates, inconsistent memory writes, or loss of historical entries. Such defects prevent the agent from maintaining a coherent temporal or contextual state. This fault can lead to failures such as context loss, temporal confusion, or incorrect recall of prior interactions. For example, <span id="S3.I8.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">camel-ai/camel</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/camel-ai/camel/pull/2119" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #2119</a>) mishandled timestamp updates during memory writes, introducing temporal inconsistency.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I8.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I8.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">State Load/Save Failure:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I8.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises when serialisation or file-system persistence of state is incorrectly implemented or constrained by invalid paths or formats. It includes improper filename construction, path-length violations, or incompatible serialisation logic. This fault can lead to failures such as inability to persist artifacts, lost state across runs, or file-system errors. For example, <span id="S3.I8.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lavague-ai/LaVague</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/lavague-ai/LaVague/issues/338" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #338</a>) generated screenshot filenames whose paths exceeded operating-system limits.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx3.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">2. Input Interpretation &amp; Logic (60 faults)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx3.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx3.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This category captures defects in parsing, validating, transforming, and semantically interpreting structured and unstructured inputs, including LLM outputs and external data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx3.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I9" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I9.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I9.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Type Handling Error:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I9.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I9.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises when dynamic inputs violate internal type expectations or when type checks are missing or incorrect. It includes unexpected <span id="S3.I9.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">None</span> values, incorrect container types, or mismatched return signatures. This fault can lead to failures such as runtime exceptions, invalid processing paths, or aborted execution. For example, <span id="S3.I9.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/issues/6098" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #6098</a>) failed when a method returned <span id="S3.I9.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">None</span> instead of the expected tuple.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I9.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I9.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Logic/Constraint Violation:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I9.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I9.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault occurs when deterministic transformation or parsing logic incorrectly processes data before or after LLM interaction. It includes incorrect parsing rules, flawed extraction logic, or violated structural constraints. This fault can lead to failures such as corrupted intermediate representations or incorrect downstream outputs. For example, <span id="S3.I9.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/pull/2262" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #2262</a>) produced erroneous table extraction due to parsing-logic defects in <span id="S3.I9.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ParsrConverter</span>.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I9.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I9.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Encoding/Decoding Error:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I9.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I9.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises when character encoding, filename encoding, or file-format handling is inconsistent with input data. It includes incorrect assumptions about character sets or improper decoding of external artifacts. This fault can lead to failures such as read/write errors or inability to process non-ASCII data. For example, <span id="S3.I9.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">langflow-ai/langflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow/issues/8482" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #8482</a>) failed when downloading projects containing Cyrillic filenames.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I9.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I9.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Validation Omission:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I9.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I9.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault occurs when required validation checks on external or model-generated data are missing or incomplete. It includes absent format checks or acceptance of structurally invalid values. This fault can lead to failures such as propagation of invalid identifiers or malformed outputs. For instance, <span id="S3.I9.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">camel-ai/camel</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/camel-ai/camel/issues/1361" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1361</a>) returned invalid arXiv URLs due to missing format validation.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I9.ix5" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I9.ix5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">File-Type Interpretation Error:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I9.ix5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I9.ix5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault occurs when the system incorrectly infers, parses, or routes an input based on its file type or content format. It includes using the wrong parser for a given extension, misclassifying MIME types, or applying inconsistent handling rules across file formats. These defects cause the agent to interpret valid artifacts using incompatible logic. This fault can lead to failures such as parse exceptions, silently incorrect extraction results, or downstream logic operating on malformed representations.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">IV. Runtime &amp; Environment Grounding (87 faults)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This dimension captures faults rooted in execution environments, including dependency stacks and platform constraints. The high frequency of these faults reflects the strong coupling between agentic frameworks and a rapidly evolving software ecosystem.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx4.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">1. Dependency Management (67 faults)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx4.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx4.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This category covers defects in specifying, resolving, importing, or installing software dependencies required for execution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx4.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I10" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I10.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I10.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dependency Specification Error:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I10.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I10.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises when declared dependency versions or constraints are incorrect or incompatible with upstream libraries. It includes outdated version pins, incompatible dependency combinations, or missing requirements. This fault can lead to failures such as import errors or broken functionality after upstream changes. For example, <span id="S3.I10.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/issues/6652" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #6652</a>) experienced import failures following an upstream structural change.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I10.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I10.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Import Resolution Failure:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I10.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I10.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault occurs when modules cannot be correctly located or loaded at runtime due to incorrect import paths or incompatible package structure. It includes invalid module references or missing submodules. This fault can lead to failures such as immediate runtime exceptions or degraded functionality. For example, <span id="S3.I10.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">langflow-ai/langflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow/pull/8457" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #8457</a>) raised warnings due to incompatible imports of <span id="S3.I10.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">astradb</span>, indicating module-resolution defects.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I10.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I10.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dependency Installation Failure:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I10.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I10.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises when environment provisioning or package installation is incorrectly configured or constrained. It includes invalid installation paths, unsupported build conditions, or packaging errors. This fault can lead to failures such as incomplete environment setup or inability to run the system. For example, <span id="S3.I10.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FoundationAgents/MetaGPT</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT/issues/1658" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1658</a>) experienced installation failures due to excessively long filesystem paths during setup.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I10.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I10.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Package Resolver/Manager Failure:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I10.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I10.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises when the package management workflow cannot correctly resolve or install a consistent set of dependencies. It includes resolver conflicts, incompatible version constraints, lockfile inconsistencies, or mismatched dependency metadata across environments. These defects prevent reproducible provisioning even when the required libraries are conceptually available. This fault can lead to failures such as dependency resolution errors, non-deterministic installations across machines, or runtime breakage caused by unexpected transitive versions. The key characteristic is that the failure originates in the resolver or package manager logic rather than in a single missing dependency.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I10.ix5" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I10.ix5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Environment Misconfiguration:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I10.ix5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I10.ix5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault occurs when runtime environment settings are incorrect or incomplete. It includes incorrect environment variables, missing system-level runtime configuration, incorrect paths (e.g., <span id="S3.I10.ix5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PATH</span>, <span id="S3.I10.ix5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PYTHONPATH</span>), or inconsistent virtual environment activation and isolation. These defects alter how dependencies are discovered and executed without changing the application code. This fault can lead to failures such as importing the wrong package version, executing against an unintended interpreter, or inconsistent behaviour across runs and hosts. Unlike dependency specification errors, this fault stems from environment-level configuration rather than declared requirements.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx4.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">2. Platform Compatibility (20 faults)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx4.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx4.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This category captures defects arising from mismatches between system assumptions and the underlying platform or external APIs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx4.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I11" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I11.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I11.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Platform Constraint Mismatch:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I11.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I11.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault occurs when assumptions about operating systems, architectures, or runtime environments conflict with the host platform. It includes hard-coded paths, architecture-specific commands, or OS-dependent behaviour. This fault can lead to failures such as missing files, execution errors, or container runtime failures. For example, <span id="S3.I11.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/issues/1382" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1382</a>) encountered file-not-found errors when running Docker containers on Apple Silicon due to architecture-specific command assumptions.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I11.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I11.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">API Compatibility Mismatch:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I11.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I11.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises when the system relies on deprecated or altered external library APIs. It includes outdated method calls or removed interfaces. This fault can lead to failures such as runtime exceptions or broken functionality after dependency upgrades. For example, <span id="S3.I11.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Netflix/metaflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/Netflix/metaflow/issues/599" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #599</a>) failed due to references to deprecated NumPy APIs.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I11.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I11.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Platform Incompatibility:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I11.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I11.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises when the system assumes platform capabilities that do not hold on the execution host. It includes unsupported operating systems, incompatible CPU architectures, missing platform features, or non-portable filesystem and process assumptions. These defects are not limited to configuration; they reflect fundamental mismatches between the implementation and the host platform. This fault can lead to failures such as runtime crashes, missing binaries, unsupported instruction errors, or container execution breakdown on particular architectures.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I11.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I11.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Integration Compatibility Mismatch:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I11.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I11.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault occurs when an integration point between components is incompatible due to mismatched versions, contracts, or expectations across systems. It includes incompatible plugin interfaces, broken adapters, or incompatible behaviour between a framework component and an external runtime or service integration layer. This fault can lead to failures such as broken end-to-end pipelines, incorrect invocation semantics at integration boundaries, or execution that succeeds in isolation but fails when components are composed.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I11.ix5" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I11.ix5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Missing Function/Interface:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I11.ix5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I11.ix5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises when the system calls a function, method, or interface that is absent in the target platform or dependency version. It includes removed methods, unavailable symbols, or missing optional interfaces that the code assumes to exist. This defect differs from general API compatibility mismatch because the immediate cause is an unavailable callable interface rather than changed semantics. This fault can lead to failures such as <span id="S3.I11.ix5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AttributeError</span>, <span id="S3.I11.ix5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ImportError</span>, or linkage errors at runtime when the missing interface is first accessed.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">V. System Reliability &amp; Observability (67 faults)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This dimension captures faults that degrade robustness, diagnosability, and user-facing reliability. In autonomous agent systems, such defects are particularly consequential because they obscure internal decision processes and hinder safe monitoring or intervention.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">1. Robustness &amp; Error Recovery (49 faults)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This category comprises defects in error detection, propagation, and recovery mechanisms whose impact is amplified by long-running agent execution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I12" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I12.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I12.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exception Handling Defect:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I12.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I12.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault occurs when exceptions are not correctly detected, propagated, or communicated. It includes swallowed exceptions, missing error reporting, or incorrect error messages that hide the underlying cause of failure. Such defects prevent reliable diagnosis and recovery. This fault can lead to failures such as silent execution errors, suppressed state violations, or undetected operation failures. For example, <span id="S3.I12.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb/mindsdb</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/mindsdb/mindsdb/pull/9659" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #9659</a>) failed to surface errors when delete operations were attempted while databases were in use, suppressing critical state information.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I12.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I12.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Defect:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I12.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I12.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This fault arises from incorrect program logic in otherwise syntactically valid code. It includes erroneous control flow, incorrect assumptions about object behaviour, or misuse of language constructs. Such defects introduce unintended behaviour even when interfaces are used correctly. This fault can lead to failures such as incorrect execution outcomes, inconsistent system state, or build and lint errors. For example, <span id="S3.I12.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crewAIInc/crewAI</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI/issues/2263" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2263</a>) reported linting failures due to improper application of object-oriented decorators.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">2. UI &amp; Visualisation Defect (13 faults)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This category captures defects in human-facing interfaces that incorrectly represent system state or execution context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A UI or visualisation defect occurs when interface elements render incorrect, incomplete, or inconsistent information about agent execution or data state. It includes missing visual updates, incorrect time or data displays, or broken interaction elements. This fault can lead to failures such as misleading system feedback, loss of situational awareness, or inability to interpret results. For example, <span id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Arize-ai/phoenix</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/issues/415" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #415</a>) failed to render selected timestamps on the embedding details page.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">3. Documentation Defect (5 faults)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This category comprises defects in developer or user documentation that misrepresent system behaviour or usage requirements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A documentation defect occurs when documentation contains incorrect, incomplete, or misleading information about system interfaces, configuration, or usage. Such defects can propagate incorrect implementation or operational practices. This fault can lead to failures such as invalid API usage, configuration errors, or misunderstanding of system capabilities. For instance, <span id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb/mindsdb</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/mindsdb/mindsdb/issues/3046" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #3046</a>) contained typographic errors in SQL API documentation that could induce invalid queries.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2. </span>Symptoms of Faults in Agentic AI Systems</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identified 13 classes of observable symptoms that represent how underlying faults manifest during agent operation. Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1.2. Symptoms of Faults in Agentic AI Systems ‣ 3.1. RQ1: What are the characteristics, observable symptoms, and root causes of bugs and failures in real-world agentic AI systems? ‣ 3. Study Results ‣ Characterizing Faults in Agentic AI: A Taxonomy of Types, Symptoms, and Root Causes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarises the frequency and distribution of these observed symptoms within the corpus of analysed fault reports.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Distribution of Bug Symptoms in Agentic Systems</figcaption>
