<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data &amp; Validation Errors (75 occurrences, 20.0%)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agents frequently encounter invalid schemas, unexpected data types, or malformed outputs when data from LLMs, tools, or external sources does not conform to expected structural or semantic constraints. Because agent workflows compose multiple transformations and generations, such inconsistencies can propagate across reasoning steps, making data validation a primary reliability bottleneck in agent pipelines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I13" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I13.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I13.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parsing Failures:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I13.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I13.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent fails to interpret structured inputs correctly and returns incomplete or null outputs instead of valid data structures. For example, in the <span id="S3.I13.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> library (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/pull/6382" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #6382</a>), the <span id="S3.I13.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">token_to_chars</span> function returned <span id="S3.I13.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">None</span> instead of the expected tuple. Such failures typically occur when internal assumptions about return signatures or intermediate representations diverge from actual tool or model outputs.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I13.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I13.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Validation Errors:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I13.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I13.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent receives or generates data that does not match the required schema or format, causing reasoning or execution steps to halt. For instance, this symptom occurred in <span id="S3.I13.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">langflow-ai/langflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow/issues/2182" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2182</a>) during integration with Azure OpenAI, where returned data violated the expected interface schema. These errors often arise at integration boundaries where external APIs evolve or LLM outputs drift from expected schemas.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I13.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I13.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Logic Errors:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I13.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I13.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent propagates invalid or inconsistent values, such as <span id="S3.I13.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">NaN</span> or undefined variables, which corrupt subsequent computations or decisions. For example, <span id="S3.I13.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb/mindsdb</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/mindsdb/mindsdb/pull/4387" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #4387</a>) exhibited <span id="S3.I13.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">NaN</span> propagation that invalidated downstream calculations. In agentic pipelines, such invalid states may contaminate memory stores or planning variables, producing persistent behavioural inconsistencies.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I13.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I13.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Security Vulnerabilities:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I13.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I13.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent exhibits unsafe behaviour, such as unintended access paths, uncontrolled resource usage, or exposure of protected data, due to insufficient validation or unsafe defaults. For example, authentication bypasses were identified in <span id="S3.I13.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">langflow-ai/langflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow/pull/6911" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #6911</a>), and memory leaks were observed in <span id="S3.I13.ix4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FoundationAgents/MetaGPT</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT/issues/868" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #868</a>). These vulnerabilities frequently emerge when unvalidated external inputs or LLM-generated parameters directly influence system actions.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Installation &amp; Dependency Issues (50 occurrences, 13.3%)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These symptoms occur when the agent system cannot be installed or configured correctly in the execution environment, preventing any subsequent operation. Their prevalence reflects the complex and rapidly evolving dependency ecosystem of agentic frameworks—spanning LLM clients, databases, and orchestration libraries—which makes deployment highly sensitive to version conflicts, missing binaries, and platform differences across environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I14" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I14.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I14.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Installation Failures:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I14.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I14.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The installation or environment provisioning process aborts due to platform constraints, missing build components, or filesystem limitations. Examples include <span id="S3.I14.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FoundationAgents/MetaGPT</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT/issues/1658" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1658</a>) failing due to excessive file path length and <span id="S3.I14.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">letta-ai/letta</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/letta-ai/letta/issues/1068" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1068</a>) failing to build <span id="S3.I14.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">faiss-cpu</span> wheels. Such failures commonly arise when packages require native compilation or assume platform features unavailable in the host environment.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I14.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I14.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Import Errors:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I14.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I14.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The system installs successfully but crashes at runtime because required modules or shared libraries cannot be located or loaded. This symptom was observed in <span id="S3.I14.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb/mindsdb</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/mindsdb/mindsdb/issues/4204" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #4204</a>), which failed due to a missing <span id="S3.I14.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">libquadmath.so.0</span> library. These errors often indicate incomplete dependency resolution or mismatches between runtime paths and installation targets.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I14.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I14.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dependency Conflicts:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I14.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I14.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent requires incompatible versions of libraries, preventing consistent dependency resolution or execution. For example, <span id="S3.I14.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">langflow-ai/langflow</span> and <span id="S3.I14.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/pull/2630" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #2630</a>) exhibited conflicts between LLM client libraries and vector database dependencies. Such conflicts frequently emerge from transitive version constraints across independently evolving ecosystem components.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Execution &amp; Runtime Failures (40 occurrences, 10.7%)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These symptoms disrupt active agent execution after deployment, indicating that the system initialises successfully but fails during task execution or component coordination. In agentic architectures, where workflows involve iterative reasoning and repeated tool or API interactions, such runtime instability can terminate long chains of operation or propagate into cascading failures across dependent steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I15" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I15.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I15.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Application Crashes:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I15.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I15.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent terminates unexpectedly during normal operation or test execution. For instance, <span id="S3.I15.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/pull/2718" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #2718</a>) failed during assistant test cases, and <span id="S3.I15.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pydantic/pydantic-ai</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic-ai/issues/1798" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1798</a>) crashed during configuration with the Gemini MCP. Such crashes typically occur when unhandled exceptions propagate through orchestration or configuration layers.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I15.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I15.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance Degradation:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I15.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I15.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent executes tasks significantly slower or consumes excessive resources due to inefficient implementation or resource handling. For example, <span id="S3.I15.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/pull/2125" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #2125</a>) experienced slowdowns caused by inefficient data-structure choices. In iterative agent loops, such inefficiencies compound across steps and may render autonomous execution impractical.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I15.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I15.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cascading Failures:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I15.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I15.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An initial runtime error triggers secondary failures that propagate through the system. For example, in <span id="S3.I15.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/pull/4389" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #4389</a>), an LLM timeout initiated retries that subsequently exhausted rate limits. These cascades commonly occur in agent pipelines where retries, memory updates, or downstream tool calls depend on earlier outputs.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Code Quality &amp; Structure Issues (38 occurrences, 10.1%)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These symptoms indicate structural deficiencies in the codebase that degrade maintainability and increase the likelihood of defects during evolution or integration. Although not always causing immediate runtime failures, such weaknesses—often revealed during linting, builds, or dependency updates—introduce hidden coupling and technical debt that destabilise rapidly evolving agent frameworks over time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I16" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I16.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I16.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Linting Errors:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I16.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I16.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Static analysis tools report violations that indicate structural or stylistic defects in the implementation. This symptom was observed in <span id="S3.I16.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crewAIInc/crewAI</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI/issues/2263" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2263</a>), where decorator misuse triggered linting failures. Such violations often signal deeper inconsistencies in abstraction boundaries or coding conventions.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I16.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I16.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deprecated Methods:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I16.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I16.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The system relies on obsolete interfaces that no longer match current library behaviour, requiring refactoring to maintain functionality. For instance, <span id="S3.I16.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PrefectHQ/marvin</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/PrefectHQ/marvin/issues/272" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #272</a>) required updates to deprecated callback managers. Continued reliance on deprecated APIs increases the risk of breakage as dependencies evolve.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I16.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I16.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Build Issues:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I16.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I16.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The build process fails or performs redundant work due to misconfigured build logic. For example, <span id="S3.I16.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">langflow-ai/langflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow/pull/4016" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #4016</a>) exhibited redundant builds, and <span id="S3.I16.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/issues/5715" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #5715</a>) failed during <span id="S3.I16.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">yarn build</span>. Such symptoms typically arise from inconsistencies between project structure, dependency configuration, and build tooling.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Agent-Specific Failures (36 occurrences, 9.6%)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These symptoms are unique to agentic architectures and arise from failures in role management, memory persistence, or multi-step orchestration. Because agents rely on probabilistic LLM behaviour and evolving internal state, defects in these mechanisms disrupt behavioural continuity and coherent operation across iterations, even when underlying components remain functional.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I17" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I17.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I17.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialization Failures:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I17.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I17.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent crashes or fails to construct internal components during instantiation. This symptom occurred in <span id="S3.I17.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/issues/2663" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2663</a>), where agents could not initialise their context. Such failures typically arise from configuration inconsistencies or component dependencies within the agent architecture.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I17.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I17.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Role Deviation and Hallucination:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I17.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I17.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent assumes incorrect identities or produces outputs that violate role or task specifications. This behaviour was observed in <span id="S3.I17.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">openai/openai-agents-python</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-python/issues/968" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #968</a>). These deviations often occur when role prompts, memory context, or orchestration constraints fail to constrain LLM generation consistently.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I17.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I17.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory and State Deficiencies:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I17.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I17.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent fails to retain or update contextual information across interactions, resulting in context loss or inconsistency. For example, <span id="S3.I17.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">camel-ai/camel</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/camel-ai/camel/pull/2119" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #2119</a>) failed to update timestamps, and <span id="S3.I17.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">eosphoros-ai/DB-GPT</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/eosphoros-ai/DB-GPT/issues/2020" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2020</a>) lost conversational state. In persistent agents, such deficiencies disrupt temporal coherence and continuity of reasoning.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I17.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I17.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Orchestration Failures:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I17.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I17.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent fails to coordinate tasks or communication among components or peer agents. An example is <span id="S3.I17.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crewAIInc/crewAI</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI/issues/1501" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1501</a>), which failed to manage inter-agent communication. These failures typically emerge in multi-agent or tool-chaining scenarios where coordination logic becomes inconsistent.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Error Handling (31 occurrences, 8.3%)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These symptoms reflect deficiencies in how agents detect, propagate, and report errors during multi-step execution. Because agent workflows span LLM inference, tool calls, and state updates, inadequate exception handling or suppressed failures can obscure causal chains, reducing observability and delaying diagnosis in autonomous operation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I18" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I18.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I18.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Failed Exception Handling:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I18.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I18.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The system fails to intercept or propagate exceptions correctly, allowing errors to escape without structured reporting or recovery. This behaviour was observed in <span id="S3.I18.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Netflix/metaflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/Netflix/metaflow/pull/1444" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #1444</a>), where escape-hatch logic bypassed normal error handling. Such failures typically occur when exception boundaries between components are inconsistently defined.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I18.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I18.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Misleading Error Messages:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I18.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I18.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The system reports incorrect or irrelevant diagnostic information that misrepresents the actual cause of failure. For example, <span id="S3.I18.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PrefectHQ/marvin</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/PrefectHQ/marvin/issues/98" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #98</a>) incorrectly attributed failures to GPT-4 access, and <span id="S3.I18.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb/mindsdb</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/mindsdb/mindsdb/issues/7162" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #7162</a>) falsely reported rate-limit conditions. Such messages often result from generic error wrapping that discards root-cause context.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I18.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I18.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unclear Error Messages:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I18.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I18.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The system emits error logs that lack sufficient context or actionable detail for diagnosis. This symptom was observed in <span id="S3.I18.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/pull/5590" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #5590</a>) and <span id="S3.I18.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Netflix/metaflow</span>, where error outputs provided no meaningful explanation of failure conditions. In agent pipelines, insufficient logging frequently obscures which step or component triggered the failure.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLM-Specific Failures (25 occurrences, 6.7%)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These symptoms arise from failures in the LLM backend that underpins agent reasoning and generation. Because the LLM functions as the agent’s cognitive core, mismatches in model capabilities, APIs, or token constraints propagate across planning and tool use, degrading reliability as prompts and outputs evolve across iterations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I19" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I19.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I19.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Token Limit Violations:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I19.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I19.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent constructs prompts or contexts that exceed the model’s allowable token window, causing request rejection or truncation. This symptom was observed in <span id="S3.I19.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/pull/4179" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #4179</a>), where prompt construction exceeded context limits. Such violations often occur when memory accumulation or tool outputs expand context beyond expected bounds.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I19.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I19.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM Incompatibility:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I19.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I19.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent attempts to use models or provider features that are unsupported or incompatible with the framework. For example, <span id="S3.I19.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">browser-use/browser-use</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/browser-use/browser-use/pull/1863" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #1863</a>) lacked support for Llama 4, and <span id="S3.I19.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">agno-agi/agno</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/agno-agi/agno/issues/3050" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #3050</a>) failed validation with Azure o3-mini. These incompatibilities typically arise when frameworks encode assumptions about model interfaces or capabilities that change over time.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I19.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I19.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Authentication Token Failures:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I19.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I19.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent fails to access the LLM service due to invalid, missing, or expired authentication credentials. This symptom occurred in <span id="S3.I19.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crestalnetwork/intentkit</span> and <span id="S3.I19.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/pull/4770" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #4770</a>). Because LLM access is foundational to agent cognition, such failures effectively disable the agent’s core functionality.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px8" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Connection &amp; Network Errors (21 occurrences, 5.6%)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These symptoms arise when agents cannot establish or maintain communication with external services such as APIs, databases, or web infrastructure. Because agent capabilities depend on remote interaction, connectivity disruptions prevent tool invocation or data retrieval, interrupting execution even when internal reasoning remains intact. Such failures often emerge from mismatches in network configuration, endpoints, or permissions across heterogeneous deployment environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I20" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I20.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I20.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Timeouts:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I20.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I20.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Requests issued by the agent exceed configured waiting limits, causing operations to abort. This behaviour was observed in asynchronous task execution within <span id="S3.I20.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Arize-ai/phoenix</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/pull/3845" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #3845</a>). Such timeouts commonly arise when external services respond slowly or when retry policies fail to account for latency variability.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I20.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I20.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Connection Refusals:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I20.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I20.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">External services actively reject the agent’s connection attempts, preventing interaction. For example, <span id="S3.I20.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/issues/617" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #617</a>) failed to connect to Elasticsearch. These refusals often indicate incorrect endpoints, unavailable services, or incompatible network configurations.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I20.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I20.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Permission Denials:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I20.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I20.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent is prevented from accessing required hardware or services due to insufficient permissions. This symptom was documented in <span id="S3.I20.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">browser-use/browser-use</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/browser-use/browser-use/issues/2571" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2571</a>), where camera and microphone access were denied. Such denials typically arise from platform security restrictions or missing authorisation settings.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px9" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">UI-related Issues (17 occurrences, 4.5%)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These symptoms affect the human-facing interface and obscure the agent’s internal state or execution outcomes, hindering monitoring and debugging. Because agents produce complex, asynchronous, and evolving state, inconsistencies between backend execution and frontend representation can conceal behaviour and reduce situational awareness in autonomous operation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I21" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I21.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I21.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UI State Inconsistencies:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I21.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I21.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The interface displays information that does not reflect the actual backend state or execution context. For instance, <span id="S3.I21.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Arize-ai/phoenix</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/issues/415" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #415</a>) showed incorrect embedding timestamps. Such inconsistencies often arise when UI components fail to synchronise with updated internal state.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I21.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I21.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Functional UI Defects:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I21.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I21.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Interactive interface elements fail to operate correctly or produce incorrect behaviour. Examples include non-functional dropdown menus in <span id="S3.I21.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">langflow-ai/langflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow/pull/3044" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #3044</a>) and incorrect tab labels in <span id="S3.I21.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb/mindsdb</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/mindsdb/mindsdb/issues/4429" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #4429</a>). These defects typically result from mismatches between interface logic and underlying system structure.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I21.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I21.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Loss:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I21.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I21.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The interface fails to display or retain previously available information, resulting in the disappearance of user-visible state. This symptom occurred in <span id="S3.I21.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">khoj-ai/khoj</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/khoj-ai/khoj/issues/382" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #382</a>), where chat history was lost. Such losses often stem from failures in state persistence or UI refresh mechanisms.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px10" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tool &amp; Function Call Issues (16 occurrences, 4.3%)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These symptoms occur during tool invocation or internal function execution, when agents translate high-level plans into concrete actions. Mismatches between LLM-generated arguments and strict interface schemas, along with evolving or incomplete tool implementations, prevent correct execution even when planning and reasoning succeed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px10.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I22" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I22.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I22.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tool Calling Errors:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I22.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I22.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent constructs or passes incorrect parameters during tool invocation, causing the call to fail. Examples include issues in <span id="S3.I22.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lavague-ai/LaVague</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/lavague-ai/LaVague/issues/338" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #338</a>) related to screenshot naming and failures in <span id="S3.I22.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crewAIInc/crewAI</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI/issues/2962" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2962</a>) during vector search. Such errors typically occur when argument schemas inferred from model outputs diverge from tool expectations.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I22.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I22.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Function Call Crashes:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I22.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I22.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Internal functions or methods invoked by the agent terminate with runtime errors during execution. This behaviour was observed in <span id="S3.I22.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/issues/6098" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #6098</a>) due to hard-coded logic assumptions. These crashes often indicate inconsistencies between orchestration logic and underlying function contracts.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I22.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I22.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unimplemented Features:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I22.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I22.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent attempts to invoke functionality that has not been implemented, resulting in explicit runtime exceptions. For example, <span id="S3.I22.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FoundationAgents/MetaGPT</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT/issues/334" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #334</a>) raised <span id="S3.I22.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">NotImplementedError</span> during tool usage. Such symptoms commonly emerge in rapidly evolving agent frameworks where interface definitions outpace implementation.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px11" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">File &amp; Resource Errors (14 occurrences, 3.7%)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These symptoms arise when agents cannot access, interpret, or persist required local or external resources such as files, templates, or stored state. Because workflows depend on consistent resource availability across environments, mismatches in paths, encoding, or storage constraints can interrupt execution and compromise continuity in stateful operation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I23" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I23.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I23.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Resource Not Found:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I23.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I23.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent attempts to access files or templates that do not exist at the specified location. This symptom occurred in <span id="S3.I23.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">letta-ai/letta</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/letta-ai/letta/issues/180" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #180</a>) and in <span id="S3.I23.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span>, where required templates or Dockerfiles were missing. Such errors typically result from incorrect path construction or missing deployment artifacts.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I23.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I23.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decode Errors:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I23.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I23.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent fails to read or process resource content due to encoding mismatches or unsupported character formats. This behaviour was observed in <span id="S3.I23.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FoundationAgents/MetaGPT</span> and <span id="S3.I23.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">khoj-ai/khoj</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/khoj-ai/khoj/issues/89" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #89</a>) when handling non-ASCII content. These errors often arise when resource encoding assumptions differ across platforms or libraries.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I23.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I23.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Upload Failures:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I23.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I23.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent fails to write data to persistent storage or remote services. For example, <span id="S3.I23.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Arize-ai/phoenix</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/issues/7941" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #7941</a>) rejected large CSV uploads. Such failures commonly occur when storage limits, file-size constraints, or API upload semantics are not correctly handled.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px12" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Documentation Issues (5 occurrences, 1.3%)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These symptoms arise when inaccurate or unclear documentation leads users or developers to misconfigure or misuse the system. Because agent frameworks expose evolving interfaces and complex configuration options, outdated or ambiguous guidance can propagate incorrect setup or integration, indirectly causing runtime or operational failures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px12.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I24" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I24.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I24.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Erroneous Guidance:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I24.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I24.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Documentation contains factual mistakes that lead to incorrect usage. This symptom was observed in <span id="S3.I24.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb/mindsdb</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/mindsdb/mindsdb/issues/3046" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #3046</a>), where SQL API instructions contained typographical errors. Such inaccuracies typically cause users to issue invalid commands or configurations.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I24.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I24.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Misleading Instructions:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I24.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I24.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Documentation provides ambiguous or confusing guidance that leads to incorrect configuration or integration. This behaviour was observed in <span id="S3.I24.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">khoj-ai/khoj</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/khoj-ai/khoj/issues/322" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #322</a>), which resulted in incorrect setup steps. These issues often arise when documentation omits constraints or assumes implicit knowledge not shared by users.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px13" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Synchronisation Issues (4 occurrences, 1.1%)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These symptoms arise from concurrency faults that disrupt coordination between parallel or asynchronous agent processes. In agentic systems with streaming, multi-agent, or concurrent tool execution, inadequate ordering or state isolation can corrupt outputs and produce inconsistent behaviour that is difficult to reproduce.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px13.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I25" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I25.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I25.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Concurrent Response Misalignment:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I25.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I25.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Outputs generated by parallel processes are interleaved or mismatched, producing incoherent responses. This symptom was observed in <span id="S3.I25.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FoundationAgents/MetaGPT</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT/pull/1381" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #1381</a>). Such misalignment often occurs when streaming or asynchronous outputs are merged without proper sequencing.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I25.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I25.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Race Conditions:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I25.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I25.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Concurrent operations interfere with each other due to improper synchronisation, leading to inconsistent or conflicting execution states. This behaviour was identified in <span id="S3.I25.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">langflow-ai/langflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow/pull/5901" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #5901</a>) during parallel task execution. These conditions typically arise when shared resources or state variables are accessed without adequate coordination.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.3. </span>Root Cause Analysis</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Across the 385 analysed faults, we identified 12 root cause dimensions that capture the underlying reasons for observed failures. While symptoms describe outward manifestations and fault types characterise structural defect classes, root causes explain <span id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">why</span> these failures occur, thereby informing prevention and remediation. This three-level taxonomy—comprising symptoms, fault types, and causes—supports a more complete account of the fault lifecycle in agentic AI systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.1.3. Root Cause Analysis ‣ 3.1. RQ1: What are the characteristics, observable symptoms, and root causes of bugs and failures in real-world agentic AI systems? ‣ 3. Study Results ‣ Characterizing Faults in Agentic AI: A Taxonomy of Types, Symptoms, and Root Causes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents the observed frequency and distribution of these root causes across the analysed fault reports.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>Distribution of Root Causes in Agentic Systems</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Root Cause Dimension</span></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Count</span></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Percentage</span></th></tr>
