</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Dependency and Integration Changes (73 occurrences, 19.5%)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Frequent changes in external libraries, platforms, and system components are the most common root cause. Agent systems rely on many evolving dependencies, such as LLM providers, databases, and frameworks. As these components change independently, they create faults in dependency setup, compatibility, and environment configuration, which later appear as installation errors, import failures, or runtime crashes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I26" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I26.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I26.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incompatible Library Versions:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I26.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I26.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when different components require versions that cannot coexist in the same environment. For example, <span id="S3.I26.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crewAI</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI/issues/1877" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1877</a>) showed incompatibility with <span id="S3.I26.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uvloop</span> on Windows, and <span id="S3.I26.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">letta-ai</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/letta-ai/letta/issues/1068" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1068</a>) failed to build <span id="S3.I26.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">faiss-cpu</span> wheels due to version conflicts. These problems often appear when libraries evolve independently and introduce clashing constraints.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I26.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I26.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Changed or Removed Library Behaviour:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I26.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I26.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise when a dependency update changes or removes behaviour that the agent code assumes will stay the same. For instance, <span id="S3.I26.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/issues/6652" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #6652</a>) hit import errors related to the <span id="S3.I26.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">send_event</span> function after a minor dependency update. These faults occur because frameworks often rely on unstated assumptions about library APIs and behaviour.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I26.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I26.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Missing or Outdated Dependencies:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I26.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I26.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when required packages or system libraries are missing or too old in the runtime environment, which prevents tools or services from starting correctly. Examples include a missing <span id="S3.I26.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">libquadmath.so.0</span> in <span id="S3.I26.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb</span> deployments (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/mindsdb/mindsdb/issues/4204" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #4204</a>) and outdated Elasticsearch connection libraries in <span id="S3.I26.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/issues/617" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #617</a>). These faults often stem from incomplete environment setup or stale installation contexts.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I26.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I26.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Platform Mismatch:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I26.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I26.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise when dependencies or compiled components assume a different operating system or hardware architecture than the deployment target. This occurred when <span id="S3.I26.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span> Docker containers (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/issues/2612" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2612</a>) failed on Apple Silicon (M2) due to architecture mismatches. Such faults are common when binaries or build pipelines target only specific platforms.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I26.ix5" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I26.ix5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hidden Dependency Conflicts:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I26.ix5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I26.ix5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when indirect (transitive) dependencies introduce constraints that are not obvious from the top-level requirements. For example, one component may rely on an older version of a library while another requires a newer version, preventing both from working in the same environment. These conflicts often come from deep dependency chains across independently maintained libraries.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx7" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Data and Type Mismatch (66 occurrences, 17.6%)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Data and type mismatches arise from differences between loosely structured data (such as LLM outputs or tool responses) and the strict data types expected by program logic. Agent pipelines constantly convert between these forms, and this mismatch causes parsing, validation, and type-handling errors that later manifest as invalid values, missing data, or execution errors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I27" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I27.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I27.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect Data Parsing:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I27.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I27.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when conversion from unstructured or semi-structured data to structured formats is implemented incorrectly or assumes a fixed structure in variable data. For example, <span id="S3.I27.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/pull/2262" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #2262</a>) failed during table extraction from unstructured text, and <span id="S3.I27.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">browser-use/web-ui</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/browser-use/web-ui/issues/475" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #475</a>) encountered JSON deserialisation errors when parsing tool outputs. These faults arise because model-generated or loosely structured data does not reliably match expected schemas.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I27.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I27.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unhandled None or Infinite Values:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I27.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I27.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise when validation or sanitisation logic does not catch undefined or extreme values before they are used in computations or logic. Instances include <span id="S3.I27.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/mindsdb/mindsdb/pull/4387" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #4387</a>) incorrectly converting <span id="S3.I27.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">NaN</span> values to <span id="S3.I27.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">None</span>, and <span id="S3.I27.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Arize-ai/phoenix</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/pull/857" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #857</a>) crashing when empty interval bins appeared in calculations. These faults occur when pipelines assume all values are valid and defined.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I27.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I27.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect Data Type Passed:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I27.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I27.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when components pass values whose types do not match expected function or interface signatures. For instance, <span id="S3.I27.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/issues/2365" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2365</a>) passed incorrect data types during custom speaker selection, causing <span id="S3.I27.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">TypeError</span> exceptions. Such mismatches typically arise from weak typing at integration boundaries or missing interface validation.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I27.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I27.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Schema–Code Mismatch:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I27.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I27.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise from a basic mismatch between dynamically generated or loosely structured data and strictly typed program logic. They occur when LLM-generated text or nullable database fields conflict with program expectations about presence, structure, or type. This reflects the gap between probabilistic data sources and deterministic code rather than a simple parsing mistake.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx8" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">LLM Behaviour and Interface Changes (49 occurrences, 13.1%)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM behaviour and interface changes come from the unpredictable outputs and evolving APIs of language models used in agents. Because prompts, parameters, and provider interfaces shape agent reasoning, variation across models and versions can introduce configuration, prompting, and model-integration issues that later manifest as incorrect outputs, token issues, or unstable reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I28" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I28.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I28.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM Misconfiguration:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I28.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I28.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when model settings are incorrect or inconsistent with provider requirements, preventing proper connection or expected behaviour. For example, <span id="S3.I28.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">letta-ai/letta</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/letta-ai/letta/issues/180" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #180</a>) used incorrect file paths for model weights, and <span id="S3.I28.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/issues/1156" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1156</a>) set improper request timeouts, causing premature disconnection. These faults arise when deployment settings do not match provider specifications.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I28.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I28.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt Formatting Errors:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I28.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I28.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise when prompt structure or control tokens are malformed or interpreted incorrectly by the model, leading to downstream processing problems. For instance, <span id="S3.I28.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Netflix/metaflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/Netflix/metaflow/pull/2047" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #2047</a>) propagated Argo tags incorrectly into the prompt context, which then caused processing errors. These faults occur because prompts mix instructions and data without strict structural separation.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I28.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I28.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Token Management Errors:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I28.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I28.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when token counting or budgeting logic does not correctly estimate or enforce context limits. For example, <span id="S3.I28.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/pull/4770" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #4770</a>) needed fixes in token-usage calculations, and <span id="S3.I28.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">agno-agi/agno</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/agno-agi/agno/issues/3620" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #3620</a>) incorrectly summed token metrics, leading to overflow errors. These faults arise because prompt growth and memory size are hard to predict in advance.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I28.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I28.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM Provider Mismatch:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I28.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I28.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise when code assumes provider-specific API formats or capabilities that differ across models or platforms. This was seen in <span id="S3.I28.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/issues/2735" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2735</a>), where <span id="S3.I28.ix4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.NET</span> tool call IDs did not match the format expected by the OpenAI provider. Such faults result from hidden coupling between agent logic and provider conventions.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I28.ix5" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I28.ix5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Configuration Sensitivity:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I28.ix5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I28.ix5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when small changes in generation parameters alter model behaviour enough to break downstream assumptions. For example, prompts tuned for OpenAI models often behave differently with Anthropic or local models due to sensitivity in <span id="S3.I28.ix5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">temperature</span> or <span id="S3.I28.ix5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">max_tokens</span>. This reflects the inherent variability of model outputs rather than a simple configuration mistake.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx9" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">State and Control Complexity (48 occurrences, 12.8%)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">State and control complexity arises because agents must maintain internal state and decision logic across many steps and interactions. Managing long-running context and adaptive behaviour is inherently difficult, and this complexity creates faults in state tracking and control flow, which later appear as inconsistent behaviour, orchestration failures, or endless loops.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I29" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I29.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I29.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardcoded Logic:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I29.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I29.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when agent behaviour depends on fixed assumptions or deterministic rules that do not adapt to changing inputs or environments. For example, <span id="S3.I29.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/issues/6098" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #6098</a>) included hardcoded assumptions in its <span id="S3.I29.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ExtractiveReader</span> that failed with different document structures. Such faults arise when static rules replace adaptive reasoning.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I29.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I29.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect Agent State Handling:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I29.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I29.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise when mechanisms that maintain or update agent state do not keep information consistent across interactions or concurrent operations. <span id="S3.I29.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FoundationAgents/MetaGPT</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT/pull/1381" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #1381</a>) mishandled concurrent requests, causing state contamination, while <span id="S3.I29.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">letta-ai/letta</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/letta-ai/letta/issues/1801" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1801</a>) failed to track agent state correctly across sessions. Such faults occur when memory or context representations become misaligned.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I29.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I29.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect Time Handling:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I29.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I29.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when scheduling or temporal reasoning logic misinterprets time values such as timezones, timestamps, or durations. This was observed in <span id="S3.I29.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">TransformerOptimus/SuperAGI</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/TransformerOptimus/SuperAGI/issues/194" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #194</a>) and <span id="S3.I29.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Arize-ai/phoenix</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/issues/415" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #415</a>), where incorrect time parsing caused logic errors. These faults arise when time semantics are oversimplified or assumed consistent across contexts.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I29.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I29.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive Control Errors:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I29.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I29.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise when the agent’s decision or control logic does not properly adjust actions to changing context or state, leading to inappropriate defaults, loops, or non-termination. Such faults typically occur when action-selection lacks feedback mechanisms or stopping conditions, reducing behavioural consistency in dynamic environments.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx10" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">External API and Tool Changes (38 occurrences, 10.1%)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">External API and tool changes occur because agents depend on external services whose interfaces and behaviour evolve over time. Since agents dynamically generate API calls and tool inputs, external changes can introduce faults in parameter handling and integration logic, which later manifest as rejected calls, malformed requests, or tool failures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx10.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I30" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I30.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I30.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect Parameter Handling:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I30.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I30.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when agents send arguments or data types that do not match the parameter schemas expected by external services. For instance, <span id="S3.I30.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SWE-agent/SWE-agent</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/SWE-agent/SWE-agent/issues/1159" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1159</a>) built API requests with incorrect parameter iteration, and <span id="S3.I30.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">langflow-ai/langflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow/pull/4386" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #4386</a>) produced invalid OpenAI client URL parameters. Such faults arise from mismatches between internal data representations and external API contracts.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I30.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I30.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect API Calls:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I30.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I30.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise when agents call the wrong endpoints, methods, or request formats that external services do not support. <span id="S3.I30.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb/mindsdb</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/mindsdb/mindsdb/pull/1061" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #1061</a>) generated malformed requests during GUI database checks, and <span id="S3.I30.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/pull/8857" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #8857</a>) attempted to access non-existent API attributes. Such faults occur when integration logic assumes outdated or incorrect service interfaces.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I30.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I30.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Integration Brittleness:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I30.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I30.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise when agent–API integrations rely on rigid or implicit structural assumptions, so that small differences in parameter order, schema, or meaning break communication. Such brittleness typically appears when validation or adaptation layers are missing, making integrations fragile and hard to diagnose or recover.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx11" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Weak Error Handling and Logging (28 occurrences, 7.5%)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Weak error handling and logging arises from insufficient mechanisms to detect, report, and manage faults during multi-step agent execution. Because agent workflows involve many components, poor exception handling and diagnostics create faults in error propagation and recovery, which later appear as silent failures, unclear messages, or cascading breakdowns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I31" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I31.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I31.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Improper Exception Handling:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I31.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I31.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when exceptions are not caught, are caught with the wrong types, or are propagated incorrectly across component boundaries. For example, <span id="S3.I31.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb/mindsdb</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/mindsdb/mindsdb/pull/9755" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #9755</a>) did not catch Langfuse errors, and <span id="S3.I31.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Netflix/metaflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/Netflix/metaflow/pull/1444" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #1444</a>) mismatched exception types during imports, leading to unhandled tracebacks. Such faults arise when exception hierarchies or integration boundaries are defined incorrectly.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I31.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I31.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unclear Error Messages:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I31.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I31.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise when error messages omit or misrepresent context needed to identify the underlying fault. <span id="S3.I31.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">eosphoros-ai/DB-GPT</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/eosphoros-ai/DB-GPT/issues/2020" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2020</a>) produced chat errors without useful context, and <span id="S3.I31.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb/mindsdb</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/mindsdb/mindsdb/issues/5427" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #5427</a>) generated misleading filters that hid the real cause. Such faults occur when logging or reporting layers lose connection to the original error source.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I31.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I31.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Suppressed Exceptions:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I31.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I31.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when exceptions are caught or ignored without restoring a consistent system state, allowing execution to continue with corrupted or incomplete data. This usually happens with overly broad catch-all handlers or missing validation after recovery, which then leads to downstream errors that hide the original fault location.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx12" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Configuration Complexity (22 occurrences, 5.9%)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Configuration complexity arises from the large number of parameters and settings that control agent behaviour and environment setup. As these configurations evolve across models and tools, inconsistencies and unclear defaults create faults in configuration specification and validation, which later appear as misconfigured components or unstable execution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx12.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I32" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I32.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I32.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Wrong Default Components:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I32.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I32.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when default configuration values select models, flags, or components that do not match the intended environment or workflow. For example, <span id="S3.I32.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">langflow-ai/langflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow/pull/5901" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #5901</a>) set incorrect concurrency defaults, and <span id="S3.I32.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepset-ai/haystack</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack/pull/4944" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #4944</a>) broke streaming LLM tracking due to default settings. Such faults arise when defaults embed hidden assumptions about execution context.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I32.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I32.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Invalid Configuration Schema:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I32.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I32.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise when configuration files or objects do not follow the structure expected by the system, often due to version changes or formatting mistakes. <span id="S3.I32.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pydantic/pydantic-ai</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic-ai/issues/1798" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1798</a>) failed with Gemini MCP schemas, and <span id="S3.I32.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb/mindsdb</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/mindsdb/mindsdb/issues/10866" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #10866</a>) crashed because of an invalid JSON comma. Such faults occur when schema definitions evolve without compatibility or validation.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I32.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I32.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Missing Configuration Validation:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I32.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I32.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when configuration inputs are not checked against expected ranges, types, or constraints, allowing invalid settings to propagate into execution. Such gaps often lead to unclear runtime errors, for example when incompatible database versions or model parameters are accepted silently. These faults arise from incomplete or absent configuration validation.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx13" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Resource and Environment Limits (16 occurrences, 4.3%)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Resource and environment limits arise when agent workloads exceed available memory, storage, or external service capacity. Agents often assume a large context and continuous execution, and this mismatch with real system limits creates faults in resource management and provisioning, which later appear as crashes, storage errors, or service interruptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx13.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I33" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I33.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I33.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">File System Limits:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I33.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I33.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when generated paths, filenames, or storage operations exceed operating system limits or violate filesystem permissions. This was observed in <span id="S3.I33.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FoundationAgents/MetaGPT</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT/issues/1658" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1658</a>), where very long file paths created during code generation caused crashes. Such faults arise when agents build filesystem artefacts dynamically without accounting for platform constraints.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I33.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I33.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory and Size Constraints:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I33.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I33.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise when data size or context length exceeds available memory or buffer capacity during processing. For example, <span id="S3.I33.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Arize-ai/phoenix</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/issues/7941" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #7941</a>) crashed during CSV uploads because the data exceeded buffer limits. Such faults occur when agents assume unlimited memory for large inputs or intermediate data.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I33.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I33.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Operational Limits:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I33.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I33.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when external resource limits—such as API quotas, latency bounds, or bandwidth restrictions—are exceeded or not handled correctly by the agent. They arise when agents lack retry, backoff, or throttling logic, for example during rate limiting or network congestion. These limits originate in external infrastructure but directly affect agent behaviour when not anticipated.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx14" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Architecture and Design Issues (11 occurrences, 2.9%)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Architecture and design issues arise from poor organisation of components, abstractions, or module structure within agent frameworks. Rapid development and integration can weaken system structure, leading to misalignment and faults in class design and module interactions that later manifest as rigidity, incompatibility, or maintenance problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx14.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I34" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I34.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I34.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Object-Oriented Principles (OOP) Misuse:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I34.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I34.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise when class hierarchies, inheritance, or encapsulation are designed incorrectly, leading to rigid or inconsistent abstractions. <span id="S3.I34.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crewAIInc/crewAI</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI/issues/2263" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2263</a>) showed structural problems in <span id="S3.I34.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CrewBase</span>, and <span id="S3.I34.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">microsoft/autogen</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/pull/5967" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #5967</a>) encountered polymorphism issues in the <span id="S3.I34.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AssistantAgent</span> class. Such faults occur when abstraction boundaries do not match actual behaviour.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I34.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I34.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Project Structure and Encoding Issues:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I34.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I34.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when module organisation or encoding assumptions do not support required functionality such as internationalisation or modular composition. For instance, <span id="S3.I34.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">langflow-ai/langflow</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow/issues/8482" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #8482</a>) failed when processing Cyrillic names, and <span id="S3.I34.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SWE-agent</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/SWE-agent/SWE-agent/issues/309" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #309</a>) showed problems in the organisation of demonstration trajectories. Such faults arise when project structure or encoding support is not general enough.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx15" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Outdated or Incorrect Documentation (10 occurrences, 2.7%)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Outdated or incorrect documentation occurs when written guidance does not match current system behaviour or configuration requirements. Because developers rely on documentation to set up agents, this mismatch leads to usage and configuration issues that later manifest as misconfiguration or integration errors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx15.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I35" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I35.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I35.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Insufficient or Incorrect Documentation:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I35.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I35.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when required usage information is missing, incomplete, or misleading, preventing correct configuration or use. <span id="S3.I35.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">julep-ai</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/julep-ai/julep/issues/45" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #45</a>) lacked essential docstrings for key methods, and <span id="S3.I35.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crewAI</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI/issues/213" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #213</a>) provided unclear instructions for setting up custom LLM environments. Such faults arise when documentation does not match current interfaces or expected parameters.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I35.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I35.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Typographical Errors:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I35.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I35.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when mistakes in documentation text cause users to copy invalid commands or code fragments. For example, typographical errors in <span id="S3.I35.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mindsdb</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/mindsdb/mindsdb/issues/3046" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #3046</a>) SQL API queries led users to run invalid statements. Such faults arise because documentation is often used directly during configuration or integration.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx16" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Access Control and Credential Issues (8 occurrences, 2.1%)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Access control and credential issues arise from incorrect permissions or authentication settings for external services and tools. Since agents operate autonomously across resources, mismanaged credentials create faults in access configuration, which later appear as denied operations or security exposure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx16.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I36" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I36.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I36.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect Permissions:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I36.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I36.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when agents are given inappropriate access rights or lack the permissions needed for intended operations. <span id="S3.I36.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">julep-ai</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/julep-ai/julep/issues/700" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #700</a>) exposed metrics endpoints incorrectly, and <span id="S3.I36.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">browser-use/browser-use</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/browser-use/browser-use/issues/2571" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #2571</a>) lacked permissions for camera and microphone access. Such faults arise when permission scopes or access policies are misconfigured.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I36.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I36.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Authentication Issues:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I36.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I36.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when authentication credentials such as API keys or tokens are missing, invalid, or managed incorrectly, preventing authorised access to services. For instance, <span id="S3.I36.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Arize-ai/phoenix</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/issues/5332" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #5332</a>) failed due to invalid tokens, and <span id="S3.I36.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">assafelovic/gpt-researcher</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/assafelovic/gpt-researcher/issues/909" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #909</a>) lacked required Tavily API keys. Such faults arise from weak secret management or missing credential validation.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx17" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Concurrency and Synchronisation Complexity (6 occurrences, 1.6%)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx17.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Concurrency and synchronisation complexity arises from coordinating parallel or asynchronous agent operations that share state or resources. Managing these interactions is difficult, and this complexity creates faults in synchronisation logic, which later appear as race conditions, deadlocks, or inconsistent state.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx17.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S3.I37" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I37.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I37.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Race Conditions:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I37.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I37.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults occur when concurrent operations access or modify shared state without proper synchronisation, leading to inconsistent or corrupted values. This was observed in <span id="S3.I37.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pydantic/pydantic-ai</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic-ai/pull/2100" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PR #2100</a>) during token refresh operations where multiple threads updated the same credential. Such faults arise when shared resources lack atomic access control.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I37.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I37.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect Synchronisation:: </span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I37.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I37.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These faults arise when coordination mechanisms are designed or applied incorrectly, leading to deadlocks, starvation, or execution errors. For example, <span id="S3.I37.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">qodo-ai/pr-agent</span> (e.g., <a href="https://github.com/qodo-ai/pr-agent/issues/1324" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Issue #1324</a>) encountered thread creation failures due to flawed synchronisation logic in its parallel processing module. Such faults occur when concurrency models do not match actual execution patterns.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx17.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS1.SSSx17.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="253.03" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 253.03" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,253.03) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 247.12 C 0 250.38 2.64 253.03 5.91 253.03 L 471.47 253.03 C 474.73 253.03 477.38 250.38 477.38 247.12 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 247.12 C 1.97 249.29 3.73 251.06 5.91 251.06 L 471.47 251.06 C 473.65 251.06 475.41 249.29 475.41 247.12 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 13.78)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:16.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="225.47" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 225.47)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS1.SSSx17.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="S3.SS1.SSSx17.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSSx17.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Summary of RQ1:<span id="S3.SS1.SSSx17.p3.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Analysis of 385 real-world faults in agentic AI systems reveals recurring patterns in <span id="S3.SS1.SSSx17.p3.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fault types</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.SSSx17.p3.pic1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">observable symptoms</span>, and their <span id="S3.SS1.SSSx17.p3.pic1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">underlying root causes</span>. Based on this analysis, we derive a taxonomy consisting of 37 fault categories organized into 13 major categories across five autonomy-related dimensions. The results show that many failures arise from interactions among LLM behaviour, state management, and external tool orchestration, and manifest as recurring symptoms such as incorrect tool outputs, inconsistent agent state, and failed task execution.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>RQ2: Statistically Significant Associations Between Faults, Symptoms, and Root Causes in Agentic AI Systems, and Their Implications for Fault Propagation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To understand how faults emerge and propagate in agentic AI systems, we applied association rule mining across four directed relationships: <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Symptom</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Root Cause</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Symptom</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fault Type</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fault Type</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Symptom</span>, and <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fault Type</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Root Cause</span>. Rules were extracted using the MLxtend library <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raschka, 2018</a>)</cite>, retaining those with confidence <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 0.20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 0.20</annotation></semantics></math> and lift <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 5.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 5.0</annotation></semantics></math> following established interestingness criteria <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2011</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Agentic AI systems are stochastic, modular, and heterogeneous. As a result, dominant failure patterns are rare. Many meaningful propagation pathways occur infrequently but consistently. A moderate confidence threshold captures these stable yet non-dominant associations, while a high lift threshold filters out spurious co-occurrence. A lift of 5.0 indicates that the antecedent and consequent co-occur at least five times more often than expected under statistical independence. The resulting rule set provides a compact, statistically grounded view of fault propagation that differs from patterns typically observed in traditional software systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Several associations are near-deterministic. Token invalidation symptoms almost always indicate failures in local token refresh or validation routines (confidence = 1.00, lift = 181.5), underscoring the fragility of long-lived session management within iterative agent loops. Incorrect timestamps and timezone inconsistencies almost always originate from naive datetime conversions in Python (confidence = 1.00, lift = 121.0), where mixing timezone-aware and naive objects contaminates scheduling, logging, and time-based reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Memory and state issues exhibit similarly strong structure. Memory-related symptoms map almost perfectly to improper state handling (confidence = 1.00, lift = 30.25), and incorrect state or memory updates consistently originate in the memory subsystem (confidence = 1.00, lift = 45.375). Because agents accumulate and reuse state across turns, corrupted state propagates forward, leading to contradictions, dropped tasks, or loss of context. Memory-related faults frequently manifest as observable memory symptoms (confidence = 0.375, lift = 27.225), making them comparatively easier to localise once surfaced.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Dependency and environment faults form another prominent cluster, particularly during installation, initialisation, and early execution. Dependency conflicts strongly predict transitive version incompatibilities (confidence = 0.600, lift = 31.114). Installation failures are often associated with deprecated tooling (confidence = 0.133, lift = 24.2) or version mismatches (confidence = 0.133, lift = 16.133). Import errors commonly arise from incorrect imports (confidence = 0.231, lift = 16.754) or missing dependencies (confidence = 0.154, lift = 11.169). These patterns reflect the asynchronous evolution of the agentic AI ecosystem, where LLM clients, vector databases, orchestration frameworks, and web stacks evolve independently. Platform heterogeneity amplifies these issues (confidence = 0.133, lift = 8.067), especially for packages with compiled components that vary across operating systems and hardware architectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A recurring multi-hop chain emerges: environment mismatch <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> import failure <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> execution failure, frequently driven by missing dependencies (confidence = 0.667, lift = 48.4). With lifts ranging from 7 to over 90, these associations indicate structural fragility rather than incidental churn.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">LLM integration faults constitute another major propagation cluster. LLM incompatibility symptoms are commonly associated with invalid credentials (confidence = 0.333, lift = 40.333), static misconfigurations (confidence = 0.222, lift = 26.889), or erroneous runtime parameters (confidence = 0.222, lift = 20.167). Invalid API keys typically halt execution immediately, whereas configuration or parameter issues surface as provider exceptions or malformed responses. Incorrect agent outputs frequently trace back to prompt formatting errors (confidence = 0.400, lift = 29.04), producing soft failures in which outputs appear superficially valid but are structurally incorrect, such as malformed JSON or invalid function signatures. LLM usage faults also propagate into adjacent layers, correlating with LLM incompatibility symptoms and data-type errors (confidence = 0.182, lift = 6.6). This demonstrates how failures at the model layer manifest as violations in program-level logic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Type and semantic mismatches arise primarily at the boundary between textual LLM outputs and strongly typed program code. Type errors are strongly associated with inappropriate data types (confidence = 0.200, lift = 18.150) and incorrect type passing (confidence = 0.200, lift = 9.075). Attribute errors often stem from incorrect parameter handling (confidence = 0.154, lift = 6.981). Incorrect or unusable outputs frequently originate from faulty data-handling logic (confidence = 0.300, lift = 27.225). These include strict boundary failures, where schema violations trigger immediate <span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TypeError</span> or <span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AttributeError</span> exceptions, as well as semantic failures in which outputs are syntactically valid but unsuitable for downstream processing. Evidence also flows in the reverse direction: data-type handling faults frequently predict type errors (confidence = 0.400, lift = 5.186), underscoring bidirectional coupling at this interface.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Insufficient observability consistently amplifies other fault types. Error-message symptoms strongly associate with unclear (confidence = 0.300, lift = 21.78) or inaccurate messages (confidence = 0.300, lift = 27.225). Exception-related symptoms predict improper handling (confidence = 0.333, lift = 30.25) and inaccurate handling (confidence = 0.333, lift = 60.5). Misleading error messages themselves emerge as a high-impact root cause <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2025</a>)</cite> (confidence = 0.667, lift = 48.4). Cascading diagnostic failures are common: defects in exception handling often produce missing or misleading messages (lifts 10.371–25.929), and logging faults correlate strongly with error-message symptoms (lift = 14.52). These breakdowns obscure tool usage, reasoning steps, and state transitions, increasing time to diagnosis and amplifying downstream propagation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Cross-layer propagation recurs throughout the results. Platform incompatibilities generate execution failures (confidence = 0.333, lift = 10.083), installation failures (confidence = 0.133, lift = 8.067), and UI anomalies (confidence = 0.182, lift = 11.0). Dependency faults manifest as conflicts, import failures, or installation errors depending on when incompatibilities surface. LLM usage faults propagate into both LLM-layer and type-related symptoms, producing cascades that originate at the model layer but surface in downstream logic. In many cases, the visible failure occurs several abstraction layers away from its root cause.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">These associations provide practical heuristics for triage. Token invalidation issues with very high lift (181.5) warrant immediate inspection of token refresh and validation logic. Timestamp anomalies (lift = 121.0) point to naive–aware datetime interactions. Memory and state symptoms (lifts 30.25–45.375) suggest defects in state transitions, persistence mechanisms, or context mapping. LLM integration symptoms with lifts between 20 and 40 justify checking credentials, configuration files, and prompt structure. Installation and early execution failures often indicate transitive dependency or platform mismatches (lifts 7–31). Type and attribute errors with lifts between 9 and 27 require scrutiny of output validation, parsing layers, adapters, and function signatures. When error messages are unclear or misleading (lifts 21–48), improving observability through structured logging, informative exceptions, and execution tracing should precede deeper debugging.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, the findings highlight architectural priorities for reliable agent design: isolating volatile dependencies behind stable adapters; enforcing strict schema validation at the model–code boundary; incorporating explicit state checkpoints and recovery mechanisms; and treating observability as a first-class design concern. The prevalence of high-lift relationships, many exceeding 20 and some surpassing 100, indicates structural propagation pathways rather than marginal effects. These signals remain stable across robustness checks: near-deterministic rules reappear consistently, dependency and LLM integration associations persist across project subsets, data-type mismatches hold across frameworks and project sizes, and observability-related lifts remain strongly significant despite moderate variability. Together, these results provide statistically grounded and actionable insight into fault propagation in agentic AI systems, informing the design of more resilient agent architectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p13.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="153.4" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 153.4" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,153.4) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 147.49 C 0 150.75 2.64 153.4 5.91 153.4 L 471.47 153.4 C 474.73 153.4 477.38 150.75 477.38 147.49 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 147.49 C 1.97 149.67 3.73 151.43 5.91 151.43 L 471.47 151.43 C 473.65 151.43 475.41 149.67 475.41 147.49 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 13.78)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:9.09em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="125.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 125.84)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.p13.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.p13.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p13.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Summary of RQ2:<span id="S3.SS2.p13.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Association rule mining identifies statistically strong relationships between fault types, observed symptoms, and root causes in agentic AI systems. Several relationships are nearly deterministic, such as token invalidation due to faulty refresh mechanisms and timestamp errors due to naive datetime handling, indicating that failures frequently propagate across state management, tool invocation, and integration boundaries.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>RQ3: To what extent does the derived taxonomy of faults align with the practical experiences and perceptions of agentic AI system developers?</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the ecological validity of the taxonomy, we conducted a structured survey of developers who are actively building and maintaining agentic AI systems. The survey asked respondents to rate the practical relevance of each fault category and to report missing or mis-specified fault patterns. This design enables validation along two complementary axes: (i) quantitative endorsement of the taxonomy’s coverage and salience, and (ii) qualitative evidence of gaps that motivate refinements.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1. </span>Quantitative Validation Results</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, the taxonomy received strong empirical validation from practitioners. Across 37 fault categories and 145 respondents, we collected 5,365 ratings using a five-point Likert scale (1 = not relevant, 5 = highly relevant). The overall mean rating was 3.97 (median = 4.0, SD = 0.87), indicating that respondents generally perceived the fault categories as relevant to highly relevant in practice. Likert-scale analyses in empirical software engineering commonly report means alongside medians and dispersion measures to characterise both central tendency and variability in practitioner judgement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boone Jr and Boone, 2012</a>)</cite>. In aggregate, 74.9% of all ratings were 4 or higher, reflecting broad endorsement of the taxonomy’s practical relevance. Only 4.4% of ratings were 2 or lower, suggesting limited disagreement with the taxonomy’s coverage. This distribution indicates consensus among respondents rather than polarised opinions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To assess whether perceived relevance exceeded neutrality, we compared the observed mean against the neutral midpoint of the scale (3.0). The mean rating was significantly higher than neutral (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>), consistent with established practices for analysing aggregated Likert responses in large-sample survey studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Norman, 2010</a>)</cite>. Together with the high proportion of ratings at 4–5, this result provides evidence that practitioners view the taxonomy as meaningfully relevant rather than marginally acceptable. Internal consistency across ratings was high (Cronbach’s <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.91" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.91</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.91</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating that respondents evaluated the categories in a coherent and reliable manner. Cronbach’s <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is widely used to assess reliability in multi-item survey instruments that measure a common underlying construct, such as perceived relevance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cronbach, 1951</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tavakol and Dennick, 2011</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dimension-Level Endorsement.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When aggregated by dimension, all five taxonomy dimensions received consistent endorsement, with mean ratings ranging from 3.90 to 4.02.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cognitive Control &amp; Orchestration (Dimension I)</em> achieved a mean rating of 3.99, reflecting the prevalence of faults related to LLM configuration and usage, API incompatibilities, token-handling issues, and authentication failures. This dimension captures failures in the cognitive layer of agentic systems, where reasoning, planning, and control are mediated through LLM interactions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Agency &amp; Actuation (Dimension II)</em> averaged 4.01, underscoring the importance of faults related to agent execution control, state management, scheduling, and termination behaviour. These failures arise when agents translate decisions into concrete actions and manage their internal lifecycle.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">External Integration (Dimension III)</em> received the highest endorsement (mean = 4.02), highlighting the centrality of tool invocation, authentication and authorisation, networking, synchronisation, and logging in deployed agentic systems. This reflects the practical challenges of coordinating agents with external services and system resources.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Perception, Context &amp; Memory (Dimension IV)</em> scored 3.95, emphasising the relevance of faults in memory subsystems, file I/O, data validation, and type handling. These issues affect how agents interpret inputs, persist context, and maintain internal state across interactions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Runtime &amp; Environment Grounding (Dimension V)</em> averaged 3.90, consistent with widespread dependency conflicts, environment reconstruction failures, and deployment instability in rapidly evolving software ecosystems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Category-Level Agreement.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At the category level, practitioners tended to assign the highest relevance to fault types that arise at system boundaries, where failures are more likely to propagate silently and complicate debugging. To complement mean ratings, we report agreement as the proportion of ratings at 4 or 5, a common operationalisation in empirical software engineering studies when assessing practitioner support for categorical constructs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Runeson et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wohlin et al., 2012</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At the category level, all fault types received generally positive evaluations, with differences reflecting the relative frequency with which practitioners encounter them. For 21 categories (55%), a large majority of respondents rated them as relevant or highly relevant, indicating that these fault types are commonly experienced across agentic AI systems. An additional 9 categories (24%) also received substantial endorsement, though with greater variation in responses, suggesting that their prominence depends on specific system designs or deployment contexts. The remaining 8 categories (21%) were endorsed less frequently, while still being viewed as relevant by many respondents. Among these, <em id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">UI and Visualisation</em> attracted the lowest level of endorsement, consistent with the fact that many agentic AI systems are deployed without interactive user interfaces and are accessed primarily through APIs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Variation by Developer Experience.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To examine whether perceived relevance varied with experience, responses were stratified by self-reported agentic AI development experience. Respondents with more than one year of experience reported a mean rating of 3.91 (72.6% high ratings), those with 6 months to 1 year reported a mean of 4.00 (75.3%), and those with less than 6 months reported the highest mean at 4.13 (81.1%). The narrow range of means across experience levels indicates that the taxonomy’s perceived relevance is stable across differing levels of practitioner expertise.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2. </span>Qualitative Validation and Taxonomy Refinements</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to ratings, respondents provided qualitative feedback on missing or under-specified fault patterns. Most respondents (88/105; 83.8%) reported that the taxonomy covered their experience, while 17 respondents (16.2%) described bug types that were not fully captured. The reported gaps cluster into five refinement themes.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(1) Non-determinism and semantic failures.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Respondents distinguished between <em id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">syntactic</em> failures (outputs violate schemas and fail fast) and <em id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semantic</em> failures (outputs are structurally valid but incorrect, inconsistent, or misaligned with ground truth). Several respondents noted that structured outputs (e.g., JSON) reduce syntax-level errors but do not prevent semantic failures (e.g., an agent asserting successful compilation when automated checks fail). These failures propagate silently through reasoning chains and often require external verifiers (tests, compilation, constraints) or human approval gates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(2) Multi-agent coordination failures.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Respondents reported failure modes specific to multi-agent settings, including misinterpretation of inter-agent messages, coordination collapse (e.g., groupthink), cascading prompt injection across agents, and higher-level deadlocks driven by circular task dependencies. While the taxonomy includes synchronisation issues, this feedback indicates a need to distinguish thread-level concurrency from <em id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">agent-level coordination</em> failures involving protocols, shared resources, and distributed task delegation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(3) Human-in-the-loop and workflow verification.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multiple respondents highlighted failures arising in systems that incorporate approval gates or verification checkpoints. Examples include incorrect escalation logic, hanging on human input, notification failures, and bypass of approval requirements. Respondents also emphasised automated workflow verification (tests, invariant checks, policy constraints) as a first-class mechanism, where failures occur when agents skip checks, misinterpret results, or fail to backtrack after verification failures.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(4) Agent-specific observability gaps.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While respondents endorsed logging as highly important, they emphasised that agent debugging requires more than conventional logs: practitioners need structured traces that capture decision steps, tool calls, and state transitions, and that remain reconstructable across multiple sub-agents. Respondents pointed to friction in adopting instrumentation tools and advocated for standardised trace schemas (e.g., thought/action/observation formats) to support replay, diagnosis, and auditing.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(5) Resource exhaustion beyond token counting.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several respondents described behavioural resource failures, such as agents failing to terminate after reaching goals, inefficient polling that inflates context windows, and runaway API usage. These issues extend beyond mechanical token counting and suggest an orchestration-level resource management refinement (budgets, backoff strategies, termination guarantees, and cost-aware control).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px5.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="122.88" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 122.88" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,122.88) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 116.98 C 0 120.24 2.64 122.88 5.91 122.88 L 471.47 122.88 C 474.73 122.88 477.38 120.24 477.38 116.98 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 116.98 C 1.97 119.15 3.73 120.91 5.91 120.91 L 471.47 120.91 C 473.65 120.91 475.41 119.15 475.41 116.98 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:6.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="95.32" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 92.63)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px5.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px5.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px5.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Summary of RQ3:<span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px5.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> A developer survey with 145 participants provides empirical support for the taxonomy. Across 5,365 ratings, the taxonomy achieves a mean relevance score of 3.97 out of 5, with 74.9% of responses indicating high relevance, suggesting that the identified fault categories closely reflect the failures developers encounter when building agentic AI systems.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis of 385 faults across 40 agentic AI repositories reveals a distinct hybrid failure profile. Unlike traditional software systems, which predominantly fail due to deterministic logic errors, or standalone machine learning models, which often fail due to data quality or model limitations, agentic AI systems fail at the intersection of both paradigms. They combine conventional software components with probabilistic, language-driven reasoning, producing failure modes that neither classical software engineering nor machine learning practice alone fully addresses. In this section, we interpret our findings through a software engineering lens and discuss their broader implications.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>The Dual Nature of Agentic Faults</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agentic AI systems cannot be characterised as purely “software” or purely “AI”; they constitute a hybrid class of systems. While cognitive failures such as hallucinations and reasoning loops are prominent in public discourse, our empirical results show that conventional software faults account for a substantial proportion of observed failures. In particular, runtime and environment grounding issues alone account for 87 cases, and logic-related faults appear in 60 instances.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This duality creates fundamental debugging challenges. Traditional debuggers assume deterministic execution and struggle with non-deterministic LLM outputs, while AI evaluation tools typically focus on output quality and overlook structural failures such as dependency conflicts, type mismatches, or state corruption. The prevalence of logic and data-handling faults highlights a deeper mismatch between probabilistic LLM outputs and rigid programming abstractions (e.g., Python’s type system). These findings suggest that existing programming languages and tooling are poorly suited to agentic communication patterns and may need to evolve toward more flexible representations and validation mechanisms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>The Integration Gap and Ecosystem Fragility</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Dependency and integration failures constitute the most common root cause in our dataset (19.5%), underscoring the fragility of the surrounding ecosystem. Agentic AI systems depend on a deep and rapidly evolving stack of components, including LLM provider APIs, vector databases, orchestration frameworks, and auxiliary libraries. Our results show that even minor changes—such as updates to a utility library or client SDK—can cascade into severe agent failures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We characterise this phenomenon as an <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">integration gap</em>: agent logic is tightly coupled to volatile external components without sufficient abstraction or insulation. This coupling renders current development practices brittle and ill-suited for mission-critical deployments. Addressing this gap will likely require stable, standardised abstraction layers that decouple agent reasoning from external services, analogous to how operating systems virtualise and manage hardware resources.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>The Observability Crisis in Autonomous Systems</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our findings also reveal a pronounced observability deficit in agentic AI systems. System reliability and error-handling faults appear 67 times, and failures frequently manifest as silent errors or opaque messages. This lack of visibility is particularly concerning because agentic systems do not merely generate text; they invoke tools, modify external state, and act autonomously in real-world environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Without clear execution traces, state snapshots, and structured error reporting, failures can propagate undetected across reasoning steps and tool interactions. These results indicate that observability should not be treated as an auxiliary concern but as a first-class architectural requirement. “Observability by design”—including structured logging, traceable reasoning steps, and auditable tool invocations—appears essential for safe and maintainable agentic systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>From Ad Hoc Debugging to Structural Heuristics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the apparent complexity of agentic failures, our analysis shows that fault propagation often follows strong and recurring patterns. Several associations are near-deterministic: token invalidation symptoms reliably indicate defects in local token-refresh logic; timestamp anomalies consistently stem from datetime conversion errors; and memory-related symptoms strongly predict faults in state-handling mechanisms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These patterns suggest that debugging agentic AI systems need not rely solely on ad hoc inspection. Instead, they enable the development of structural heuristics and automated checks that guide diagnosis. For example, static or runtime analysers could flag high-risk patterns (e.g., naive datetime usage, unvalidated state updates) before deployment, reducing time-to-diagnosis and failure impact.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5. </span>Implications for Software Engineering Research</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, our findings highlight several implications for software engineering research. Classical unit testing is insufficient in settings where core components are stochastic and environments evolve rapidly. We identify multiple promising research directions, including: (i) mock-free integration testing frameworks that simulate realistic tool interactions without brittle assumptions; (ii) probabilistic or schema-flexible type systems that better accommodate unstructured LLM outputs; and (iii) self-healing agents capable of detecting failures, diagnosing root causes, and executing structured recovery strategies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, while agentic AI systems promise a new class of autonomous software, our study shows that the supporting engineering foundations remain fragile. Progress from <em id="S4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">faults</em> to <em id="S4.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fixes</em> will require treating agentic applications as complex, probabilistic systems that demand dedicated software engineering principles, tools, and abstractions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Threats to Validity</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Threats to <em id="S5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">internal validity</em></span> arise from potential errors or biases in both manual analysis and automated filtering. Manual annotation introduces subjectivity, which we mitigated through batch-wise analysis, an iterative coding process, and regular consensus discussions to resolve disagreements. Another threat concerns the accuracy of automated filtering performed using GPT-4.1. To address this, we evaluated the model against a human-labelled ground truth, achieving 83% accuracy and 97% recall, and manually inspected a representative subset of predictions. Finally, association rule mining may surface spurious correlations; we mitigated this risk by retaining only high-lift rules (<math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{lift}\geq 5.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>lift</mtext><mo>≥</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{lift}\geq 5.0</annotation></semantics></math>), prioritising strong structural dependencies over coincidental co-occurrences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Threats to <em id="S5.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">construct validity</em></span> concern whether the taxonomy and dataset accurately capture real-world faults in agentic AI systems. To reduce noise and speculative reports, we retained only closed issues and merged pull requests, increasing confidence that the faults were reproducible and verified. Taxonomy completeness is another potential threat. We mitigated this by conducting a practitioner survey with developers across diverse frameworks and experience levels, which confirmed strong alignment with real-world fault patterns while also informing targeted refinements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Threats to <em id="S5.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">external validity</em></span> relate to the generalisability of our findings. Our analysis focuses on Python-based repositories, which may limit applicability to other ecosystems; however, Python currently dominates agentic AI development and deployment. To improve representativeness, we employed stratified sampling across four repository types (frameworks, libraries, tools, and applications). Additionally, we restricted selection to repositories with more than 1,000 stars and at least 30 issues, favouring mature, actively maintained projects over short-lived experimental artifacts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Taxonomies of failures in agentic AI systems.</span>
The increasing deployment of agentic workflows has motivated several efforts to categorise failures in such systems. Existing taxonomies generally adopt one of three perspectives: platform-centric, interaction-centric, or task-centric. From a platform-centric viewpoint, Ma et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025</a>)</cite> classify failures arising from orchestration infrastructure that manages agent lifecycles, demonstrating how platform-level constraints can cascade into system-wide crashes. In the context of multi-agent systems, Cemri et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cemri et al., 2025</a>)</cite> take an interaction-centric approach, analysing emergent failures such as consensus loops and hallucinated communication protocols during inter-agent coordination. Task-centric taxonomies, including those proposed by Lu et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and Rahardja et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rahardja et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, categorise failures based on task outcomes; Rahardja et al., in particular, focus on logic errors and syntax violations in self-debugging agents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While these studies provide valuable high-level perspectives, they primarily characterise failures in terms of execution context or task success, rather than grounding them in the underlying software architecture that implements agent behaviour.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Failure attribution and diagnosis in agentic systems.</span>
Complementary to taxonomic efforts, recent work has examined how failures can be attributed and diagnosed in agentic workflows. Zhang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> propose a voting-based consensus mechanism to identify which agent caused a failure in multi-agent tasks, addressing the challenge of blame assignment in non-deterministic settings. Zhu et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> explore self-reflective feedback loops in which agents analyse their own execution traces to refine prompts and reasoning strategies. Although effective for improving task-level performance, these approaches largely treat agents as black boxes, emphasising actor attribution or prompt refinement rather than diagnosing faults in internal software components.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Positioning of this work.</span>
In contrast to prior studies, our work focuses on the architectural and empirical roots of failures in agentic AI software. First, we ground failures in concrete internal components—such as <span id="S6.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">memory subsystems</span>, <span id="S6.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">planning and control logic</span>, and <span id="S6.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tool interfaces</span>—shifting the analytical focus from “who failed” to “what broke.” Second, whereas existing datasets are often benchmark- or task-specific, we analyse 385 real-world faults spanning frameworks, libraries, tools, and applications, providing coverage across the agentic AI software stack. Third, we apply Apriori-based association rule mining to quantify statistically significant relationships between symptoms, fault types, and root causes, exposing recurrent fault-propagation pathways. Finally, we validate the resulting taxonomy through a structured practitioner survey, ensuring alignment with the experiences and mental models of developers actively building and maintaining agentic AI systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Conclusion and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agentic AI systems exhibit distinctive failure modes that arise from the interaction of probabilistic LLM behaviour, autonomous control loops, tool-mediated actuation, and rapidly evolving software ecosystems. This paper presents a large-scale, architecture-grounded empirical study of bugs in agentic AI applications. We analysed 13,602 closed issues and merged pull requests across 40 widely used repositories and conducted in-depth manual analysis of 385 representative faults. Using a grounded-theory approach, we derived taxonomies of fault types, symptoms, and root causes, and mapped them to core architectural components, including cognitive control and orchestration; agency and actuation; perception, context, and memory; runtime and environment grounding; and system reliability and observability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our association rule analysis revealed high-lift fault-propagation pathways, including token invalidation symptoms pointing to token-refresh defects, timestamp anomalies indicating misuse of datetime abstractions, memory-related symptoms predicting state-handling failures, and installation or import failures stemming from dependency and platform incompatibilities. Together, these results show that bugs in agentic AI systems differ structurally from those in traditional software: failures frequently cross boundaries between model behaviour and program logic, propagate through stateful control loops, and are amplified by fragile dependency stacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These findings have several implications. The prevalence of many-to-many symptom–cause relationships highlights the limitations of component-local debugging and underscores the need for architecture-aware triage and observability by design. Robust agentic systems require explicit contracts at the model–code boundary, systematic state management across long-horizon executions, and insulation from ecosystem volatility.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Future work will extend this study along several dimensions. We plan to broaden the dataset and taxonomy across programming languages, execution environments, and deployment contexts. We will investigate provider-agnostic adapters with compatibility checking, stronger model–code contracts with validation and graceful degradation, and state-safety mechanisms that support recovery in long-running control loops. We also aim to develop self-diagnosing and self-healing agents, improve observability through standardised telemetry and execution traces, and construct realistic testbeds and reliability benchmarks for agentic systems. Finally, we plan to release public benchmarks and explore automated repair techniques that map observed symptoms to likely root causes and corrective actions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By systematically characterising fault types, symptoms, and propagation pathways in real-world agentic AI software, this work contributes empirical foundations and actionable guidance for improving the reliability, diagnosability, and trustworthiness of next-generation autonomous systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
